-- ======================================================================
--  Immersive Quest Markers: the card window and everything drawn in it
-- ======================================================================
-- The VIEW. IqmCards is one HUD dialog holding three fixed pools of widgets -- the
-- route's ground marks, the cards, the markers -- positioned per frame by whoever
-- has decided what should be on screen. Nothing here decides anything: it is handed
-- a slot index, a screen point and a colour, and it puts a sprite there.
--
-- Split out of iqm_core at R2.45, the largest of the three pieces and the easiest
-- to reason about afterwards, because it is the one with no state that outlives a
-- frame beyond the widgets themselves. The reason for splitting at all is the reason
-- iqm_debug was split out at R2.44: a .script gets 198 locals, going over is a
-- SYNTAX ERROR AT LOAD, and iqm_core had reached 187 of them.
--
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE SEAM (see the long version in iqm_core, under "THE SHARED-STATE SEAM")
--
-- CONFIG IS PULLED. iqm_core.read_config ends by calling apply_config() below,
-- which reaches over for the handful of options the look depends on and folds them
-- into RTE / RD_G. Everything else arrives as an ARGUMENT: draw_slot and draw_beacon
-- both take `cfg` and always have, which is why this file holds no `C` at all.
--
-- NOTHING IS REFERENCED AT FILE SCOPE. iqm_core reads this module back, so both
-- halves of that pair bind in on_game_start / apply_config, where every .script has
-- finished loading. (iqm_util is the exception that proves it: nothing reads iqm_util
-- back, so it is safe to read at file scope -- see the note in that file.)
--
-- THE INSTANCE lives here with the class, because `class` and its constructor belong
-- together and lifecycle got the smaller half: ensure() builds the dialog and hands
-- it back, and iqm_core caches what it gets so its per-frame path is not a
-- namespace lookup eight times a frame. There is exactly one dialog and the HUD holds
-- the real reference (AddDialogToRender + SetAutoDelete); the two names for it here
-- and there are the same object, never two.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------

-- How many card widget-sets to build. iqm_core' own cap, restated here rather than
-- read across the seam because a widget pool is built once, at init, from a number
-- that has never changed at run time -- and reading it lazily would mean a pool sized
-- before the module holding the number had loaded. Change one, change both; the slot
-- harness reads MAX_CARDS out of iqm_core, which is the copy that decides.
local MAX_CARDS     = 8
-- Most markers on screen at once. Raised from 4 (R2.39): a real hub reaches five
-- legitimate candidates -- Rostok had three turn-ins, a technician and a guide all inside
-- beacon_dist -- and at 4 the fifth was dropped with nothing to say so. The cap is a
-- legibility limit, not a budget one: the draw cost is a handful of widgets either way.
-- A NAMESPACE FIELD, not a local: iqm_beacon caps its draw at this and must read the
-- same number the widget pool was built to. It is only read here at init and on a
-- config change, so being a namespace lookup costs nothing on this side.
MAX_BEACONS   = 6            -- most objective NPCs marked at once (several tasks can be at hand-in)
-- Characters in a marker's range readout. Three digits and the suffix: beacon_dist stops
-- at 200, and the placed waypoint -- the one marker with no distance limit -- is clamped
-- to 999 m in draw_beacon rather than given a fifth widget for the rare walk across two
-- levels' worth of map.
local MAX_RGLYPH    = 4
-- The range readout's art, one texture id per character (see iqm_digits in
-- iqm_textures.xml). Indexed 0-9 so a digit indexes it directly, with no arithmetic on
-- the draw path; RD_G carries the proportions, generated by tools/digit-tex/build.py.
local DIGIT_TEX = { [0] = "iqm_digit_0", "iqm_digit_1", "iqm_digit_2", "iqm_digit_3",
                    "iqm_digit_4", "iqm_digit_5", "iqm_digit_6", "iqm_digit_7",
                    "iqm_digit_8", "iqm_digit_9" }
local RD_G = {
	-- Readout height as a fraction of beacon_size: THIS is the "font size", and being a
	-- number rather than one of the engine's eleven fonts is the whole point of drawing
	-- the readout as sprites. read_config overwrites it from beacon_rsize; the value
	-- here is only the pre-config default. 0.40 of the default 14-unit glyph is ~10.5
	-- screen px at 1440p, against 16 for font="small" and 22 for arial_14.
	f   = 0.40,
	-- Generated by tools/digit-tex/build.py -- rerun it and re-paste if the art changes.
	-- These are proportions of the DECLARED RECT, which includes each glyph's baked
	-- keyline (R2.31), so the figures themselves are 104/126 of the readout height.
	w   = 0.6667,  -- a digit's width, as a fraction of the readout height (tabular)
	mw  = 0.7698,  -- the metre suffix's width
	mh  = 0.7540,  -- ...and its height: an x-height glyph, so shorter than a figure
	mdy = 0.2460,  -- ...so it drops by this much to sit on the figures' baseline
	-- Tracking is NEGATIVE, which looks wrong and is right: each rect now carries an
	-- 11px keyline on both sides, so butting two rects together would leave 22px of
	-- transparent margin between the figures -- visibly loose at a 10px readout. This
	-- pulls the boxes back so the keylines just meet. Where they overlap, both are the
	-- same black, so the join is a rim rather than a seam.
	trk = -0.08,
	sp  = 0.10,    -- the extra gap before the suffix, in place of a space glyph
}

-- Route sprite opacity, read once per frame by the route draw. Unlike the wire
-- renderer this one HAS alpha blending, so it is a real control rather than an
-- inert slider -- which is why the route gets an opacity option and the old
-- ribbon deliberately did not.
-- Everything the route draw needs, in ONE table on purpose.
--
-- A .script file's body is a single Lua function, and Lua 5.1 gives a function a
-- hard limit of 200 LOCALS (198 here -- the engine prepends a two-local namespace
-- header). Going over is not a warning or a slow path: it is a SYNTAX error raised
-- at load, so the whole module fails and the cards and beacon go with it -- which is
-- exactly what happened when the route became a stroke and added nine more. Note
-- LuaJIT's limit is higher, so an offline `luajit -bl` check compiles this file
-- happily; tools/check-lua.py is what actually catches it.
--
-- THIS FILE IS NO LONGER AT THAT CEILING -- R2.45 split iqm_core three ways and
-- the drawing landed here with room to spare -- but the grouping stays, and not out
-- of inertia. Twenty loose names for one renderer's state is worse to read than one
-- table whatever the budget says, and the file this table was pressed flat for is
-- still the one that would break first if it came back apart.
--
-- So route state is grouped rather than spread over twenty names. The hot fields
-- are hoisted into function locals at the top of draw_route, which is the usual
-- Lua idiom and costs nothing.
--
-- Style values are refreshed by apply_config, which pulls them from iqm_core'
-- live config; the colour and nominal sizes are kept apart from the accent (see the
-- note in iqm_core' DEFAULTS).
local RTE = {
	a = 215, r = 134, g = 152, b = 86,   -- opacity and colour

	MARKS = 16,    -- ground glyph pool: one quad per chevron, so iqm_nav's MAX_CHEV exactly

	-- Ground chevron geometry, in METRES of ground -- the same currency as `ww`, because
	-- these are drawn from the same measured-thickness machinery. Trimmed from
	-- 0.95/1.30/0.28 after the tenth session: at 3 m spacing the marks want to read as a
	-- band rather than as a row of slabs, and the nearest one gets its full perspective
	-- size, so it is the near end that sets the ceiling on all three.
	--
	-- Grown again in R2.26, when `marks` made the chevrons the whole route: a mark sized
	-- to ride a stroke is undersized once there is no stroke to ride. `awide` is what
	-- lengthens the ARMS (an arm runs sqrt(hl^2 + hw^2) from the tip to a back corner, so
	-- the width across the back is its long dimension) while `alen` is unchanged, which
	-- is what keeps the result a chevron and not a dart.
	--
	-- These MIRROR MARK_LEN / MARK_WIDE / MARK_ARM in tools/stroke-tex/build.py, which
	-- draws the one-piece glyph in this same metric. Change one, change both: nothing
	-- checks it, and a mismatch shows as the glyph sitting slightly off its own box.
	-- (tools/route-preview applies the rule automatically, so a sweep there is a way to
	-- try a size before committing it to two files.)
	--
	-- Taken down 20% in R2.30 (0.85/1.85/0.38), the first time this size was judged in the
	-- GAME rather than in the preview: a mark 1.85 m across is a metre wider than the
	-- doorway it walks you through. All three scale by the SAME factor on purpose -- the
	-- glyph's box is then exactly similar, so the artwork still fits it and iqm_mark.dds
	-- does not need rebuilding. Scale one alone and the picture no longer matches its rect.
	--
	-- `alen` ALONE carries the keystone (R2.31). It is the mark's depth along travel --
	-- the near edge of the footprint and the far edge sit at different distances, and
	-- that difference is the whole of what a rotated rectangle cannot express. Measured:
	-- halving it cuts the worst corner error a third; halving `awide` instead changes it
	-- by 1%. So a SHALLOWER chevron sits flatter against the floor, and this is 0.68
	-- flattened to 0.50.
	--
	-- TO REVERT: set this to 0.68 and MARK_LEN in tools/stroke-tex/build.py to match,
	-- then run `python tools/stroke-tex/build.py` to redraw iqm_mark.dds. Nothing else
	-- moves -- alen is the only number that changed, and the texture is derived from it.
	alen  = 0.50,  -- tip to the back of the arms, along travel (the DEFAULT shape's)
	awide = 1.48,  -- across the back, tip to tip
	aw    = 0.304, -- thickness of an arm

	-- THE SELECTABLE SHAPES (R2.32, cut to three in R2.43). `route_shape` in MCM keys
	-- this table.
	--
	-- KEYED, NOT A SEQUENCE, and the gaps are deliberate. These numbers are what MCM has
	-- already written into players' ltx files, so 3, 7 and 8 go on meaning exactly the
	-- shapes they have always meant. Renumbering the survivors 1-3 would have been tidier
	-- to read and unmigratable in practice: a stored 3 would then mean chevsharp where it
	-- used to mean arrow, and nothing in the ltx says which version wrote it. The five
	-- retired numbers simply stop resolving, and read_config sends them to the default --
	-- so use `RTE.SHAPES[n]`, never `#RTE.SHAPES` or ipairs, on a table with holes in it.
	--
	-- The five that went (chev50, chev30, rung, square, dart) were the alignment half of
	-- the R2.31 trade, and the trade turned out to be one-sided on the floor: the flatter
	-- shapes lie better and say less, and a mark that does not point is a mark the player
	-- reads as decoration. What is left is one arrowhead and two chevrons at different
	-- sizes. Their cells are still IN the atlas -- see tools/stroke-tex/build.py, which
	-- still draws all eight so the committed iqm_marks.dds stays valid.
	--
	-- The survivors, with the worst corner error over a 1.5 m strafe as a fraction of the
	-- mark's own drawn width (R2.31-R2.32; the two chevrons were never re-measured, so
	-- their rows carry the ratio the percentage tracks rather than an invented number):
	--
	--     3 arrow     depth 0.52   17% at 5 m, 10% at 9 m   points
	--     7 chevtight depth 0.67   ratio 0.68               points   (the DEFAULT)
	--     8 chevsharp depth 0.67   ratio 0.89               points, hardest
	--
	-- `tex` is a cell of ui\iqm_marks, and `kind` picks the box formula in shape_box --
	-- which MUST agree with shape_box() in tools/stroke-tex/build.py, since that is what
	-- drew the picture. The old change-one-change-both rule.
	--
	-- `awide` and `aw` are OPTIONAL per-row overrides of the family's width and stroke
	-- weight, added in R2.40 for shape 7 and absent on the arrow. They exist because a
	-- chevron's apex angle is not a parameter of its own: it is atan(awide / 2*alen), so
	-- "the same mark with a tighter point" is unsayable without moving one of the two
	-- numbers this table otherwise holds constant across the family. Leave them out unless
	-- a shape genuinely needs its own -- the build's agreement check compares an absent
	-- field against an absent field, so spelling out the family value is a mismatch.
	SHAPES = {
		[3] = { tex = "iqm_mark_arrow",  kind = "arrow",   alen = 0.50 },
		-- 7 chevtight, "Chevron - large": 0.90 m across against the family's 1.48 and
		-- 0.42 deep against the retired chev50's 0.50, which closes the apex from 112
		-- degrees to 94; the arm thins to match, since arm length scales with the width
		-- and the family weight on a mark this size is a 2:1 blob rather than a chevron.
		-- "Large" is relative to shape 8 alone -- it is the bigger of the two chevrons
		-- left, not a big mark. The trade it makes goes the wrong way and is the point of
		-- the entry rather than a flaw in it: depth over width is 0.68 against chev50's
		-- 0.56 (measured, off the atlas build), and that ratio IS the keystone residual
		-- (R2.31) -- a tighter point and more depth per width are the same fact, so this
		-- one lies about a fifth less flat than the mark it replaced as the default.
		[7] = { tex = "iqm_mark_chevtight", kind = "chevron", alen = 0.42, awide = 0.90, aw = 0.22 },
		-- 8 chevsharp, "Chevron - small": tighter again, about 68 degrees. The WIDTH is
		-- what closed it, not the depth -- a 68-degree apex at shape 7's width would need
		-- alen 0.62, deeper than anything else in the family, and depth is the keystone
		-- residual.
		--
		-- It costs anyway, and by more than guarding the depth suggested: depth over width
		-- comes out 0.89 (measured off the atlas build), against shape 7's 0.68 and
		-- chev50's 0.56. Narrowing raises that ratio exactly as deepening does -- it is
		-- depth over WIDTH, and only the numerator was being watched. Small, too: 0.62 m
		-- across reads as a tick rather than a mark at any distance. The angle is what it
		-- is for.
		[8] = { tex = "iqm_mark_chevsharp", kind = "chevron", alen = 0.46, awide = 0.62, aw = 0.18 },
	},
	-- HOW MANY PICTURES OF EACH SHAPE (R2.42). Every mark used to be the same bitmap, so a
	-- run of them repeated one scuff and one camo blotch at even spacing -- which is what a
	-- repeated texture looks like once there are a dozen on screen, and the conveyor put a
	-- dozen on screen. The variants differ ONLY in their noise: same geometry, same box,
	-- same keyline, because place_mark measures one box per shape (RTE.SB) and a variant
	-- with its own outline would need its own.
	--
	-- Picked by HASHING the mark's stable id rather than cycling it. A cycle at three
	-- variants reads as ABCABC down the run, which is a pattern where the point was to
	-- break one; a hash of the same id is stable per mark (so a mark keeps its picture as
	-- it flows toward you) and looks unordered along the run.
	--
	-- Must equal MARK_VARIANTS in tools/stroke-tex/build.py -- that is what drew the cells,
	-- and asking for a variant it did not draw is a missing texture on every third mark.
	NVAR = 3,
	-- The suffix each variant's texture id carries. Variant 1 is the bare id, so a
	-- single-variant atlas needs no rename and iqm_strip's carved bands are untouched.
	VSUF = { "", "_b", "_c" },
	-- The active shape's box, filled by read_config so place_mark does no per-mark
	-- geometry. back/fwd are asymmetric for the chevron alone (it holds a mitre).
	SB = { back = 0, fwd = 0, half = 0 },
	-- (There is no MITRE any more. R2.18's overlap, R2.19's asymmetric version and the
	-- projected-angle fix that followed are all gone: the apex is left to iqm_arm's taper.
	-- See place_arms, and R2.22 in docs/ar-navigation.md before adding another.)
	--
	-- Feather allowance around the glyph's bounding box, as a fraction of an arm's
	-- thickness. MUST match FEATHER in tools/stroke-tex/build.py: it is how much room the
	-- artwork leaves itself outside the chevron's true outline, and place_mark has to
	-- include the same margin in the box it measures or the glyph lands off its own rect.
	FPAD  = 0.04,
	-- Shadow allowance, in the same currency: how far outside the glyph's true outline
	-- the BAKED contact shadow reaches, as a fraction of an arm's thickness (R2.33).
	--
	-- Same contract as FPAD and the same failure if it drifts -- room the artwork uses
	-- that the renderer has to measure. Here the failure is louder: crop a soft shadow at
	-- the rect's edge and you get a hard straight line across it, on every mark. Mirrors
	-- MARK_SHADOW_W * 0.5 in tools/stroke-tex/build.py, which refuses to build the atlas
	-- on a disagreement and prints the value to put here. 0 when the shadow is off.
	SPAD  = 0.375,
	-- Stroke width the dense styles use in place of route_w, since a chevron every 3 m
	-- carries the path and the stroke is only there to join them up.
	bandw = 0.30,

	-- Chevron sizing. A billboard's on-screen size is its world size over its
	-- distance, so scaling by REF/d is what makes a receding run of them read as
	-- marks lying along a path rather than a row of equal glyphs. The clamps stop the
	-- nearest swallowing the screen and the farthest collapsing to a flickering
	-- sub-pixel speck.
	--
	-- The STROKE is not sized this way any more (R2.13). Its thickness is measured
	-- from the projection of its own ground edges, which is the only way to get the
	-- grazing-angle foreshortening a band on the floor actually has; REF plays no
	-- part in it and WMAX is a guard against a bad projection, not a look control.
	REF   = 12.0,  -- metres at which a chevron is drawn at its nominal size
	MIN   = 5,     -- px: below this a chevron is scintillation, not a marker
	MAX   = 44,    -- px
	HALO  = 3,     -- px the black backing copy extends past a chevron
	WMIN  = 2.5,   -- px: the stroke stays at least this thick at any range
	WMAX  = 96,    -- px: sanity ceiling only (see above)
	-- The chevron GLYPH's own ceiling. Its box is 1.2 m across where the band is 0.30, so it
	-- passes WMAX legitimately at close range -- and clamping there squashed the glyph while
	-- its length went on being measured, which changed its apparent ANGLE with distance
	-- (R2.20). The UI space is 768 high, so this is "taller than the screen", i.e. a real
	-- sanity limit rather than a look control.
	MCAP  = 768,
	-- Dark halo on each side of the stroke, as a FRACTION of its thickness and clamped
	-- in px. Fixed at 2 px it was invisible on a 90 px near segment and the dominant
	-- feature of a 3 px far one. Kept BELOW the ~14% thickness step between
	-- neighbouring segments (SEG_DIV in iqm_nav) so a thin segment's halo does not
	-- stick out past its thicker neighbour's body -- see place_seg.
	-- Tightened and darkened in R2.26 (0.16 / 0.55). A wide halo at just over half alpha
	-- is a GLOW, and a glow around a mark is the thing that reads as blur -- it puts a
	-- gradient exactly where the eye looks for the edge. Narrower and denser is a
	-- keyline: it states where the mark stops instead of suggesting it, and against pale
	-- concrete it separates better at half the width. Same trade the map icons made.
	EDGE  = 0.10,
	EDGE_MIN = 1.25,
	EDGE_MAX = 5,
	SHA   = 0.75,  -- halo alpha, as a fraction of the stroke's own

	-- Near-end fade, the mirror of FADE/TAIL below. The stroke starts NEAR_TRIM metres
	-- out (iqm_nav) and used to start there at full strength, which is a wide band
	-- stopping dead across the bottom of the screen.
	--
	-- In METRES of camera distance, not as a fraction of the vertex list (R2.15). As a
	-- fraction it was 0.14 of ~24 vertices, and with angle-based spacing the near
	-- vertices are the SHORT ones -- so it covered about the first 3 m of path at as
	-- little as a tenth of the stroke's alpha, on top of the 3 m trim. The route was
	-- invisible for its first 6 m and looked like it started 8 m away, which is the
	-- complaint NEAR_TRIM was already accused of. A distance is also the honest variable:
	-- what wants softening is the stroke's arrival at the near plane, which is a fact
	-- about the camera and not about how many vertices the route happens to have.
	-- 3.5 m rather than 2: with ground chevrons the nearest mark is the biggest thing on
	-- screen by a wide margin, and the fade is the only thing keeping it from dominating
	-- the frame. It cannot be fixed by shrinking the mark -- that would shrink it at
	-- every distance -- and shortening the perspective is not an option, so the near end
	-- is simply held back.
	-- 3.5 m / 0.30 until R2.26, and both were sized against a NEAR_TRIM that no longer
	-- exists. R2.25 took the trim to zero so the route starts at your feet -- which means
	-- the first few metres, previously not drawn at all, are now drawn INSIDE this fade.
	-- The gap became a ghost: at 215 alpha and 0.30 the nearest mark reached the screen
	-- at a quarter of full strength, which is most of what "too much blur" turned out to
	-- be. There is a second reason to flatten it: alpha is per-widget and a quad cannot
	-- carry a gradient, so a steep ramp lands as a STAIRCASE -- measured at 64, 79, 110,
	-- 148, 188 over five consecutive segments, i.e. 35% jumps, which read as banding
	-- rather than as a fade. 1.5 m and 0.75 keep the near plane softened without either.
	HEAD  = 1.5,   -- metres beyond the nearest drawn vertex over which the fade runs
	HMIN  = 0.75,  -- alpha multiplier at the very near end

	-- THE NEAR CUT-OFF, back from R2.21 and for exactly the reason it existed there.
	--
	-- A mark is ONE quad, and a quad's fit is affine: it can be rotated and scaled on two
	-- axes, so it can be a parallelogram, where the true projection of a patch of ground
	-- is a TRAPEZOID. That holds while the patch is small enough for perspective to be
	-- locally linear, and a mark three metres from the eye is not: it spans real depth,
	-- its far edge genuinely is narrower than its near edge, and a parallelogram cannot
	-- say so. The residual reads as skew -- and since it depends on where the mark sits
	-- relative to the vanishing point, it SWINGS AS YOU MOVE.
	--
	-- That is the whole of "they rotate when the player moves", reported in the fifteenth
	-- session and again in the nineteenth. It is not a heading error, which is why
	-- smoothing the world direction (iqm_nav's CHEV_DIR) did not touch it: the direction
	-- was right and the drawing of it was wrong. R2.22 removed the cut-off along with the
	-- glyph; R2.26 made the glyph the only design, so it comes back with it.
	--
	-- Below MNEAR nothing is drawn. Not held at a low alpha -- R2.19 tried that and the
	-- honest move is not to draw a mark you cannot draw correctly. Nothing is lost: a mark
	-- three metres from your eye has already told you which way to walk.
	-- THE RAMP WAS PERMANENTLY GHOSTING ONE MARK (R2.33b), and for a reason that had
	-- stopped being true. It was 3.0 / 2.0 -- a 2 m ramp from the cut-off -- and the note
	-- justifying its width said: "ramped rather than switched, because a world-anchored
	-- mark crosses this threshold as you walk and a hard edge there would pop."
	--
	-- Marks stopped being world-anchored in R2.29. Each one now holds a FIXED distance
	-- ahead of the player (s_actor + k * gap), so it does not cross the threshold: it
	-- PARKS wherever its own k puts it and stays there. At the default 3 m spacing the
	-- first mark parks at 3.29 m of camera distance, which is 14% of the way up a ramp
	-- that ends at 5.0 -- so it drew at 37 of 255 for as long as the route was up, with
	-- the next mark at full. Measured across every spacing the menu offers:
	--
	--     gap 2  -> first drawn mark at 4.22 m,  61%
	--     gap 3  -> 3.29 m,  14%        <- the default, and the one reported
	--     gap 4  -> 4.22 m,  61%
	--     gap 5+ -> clear
	--
	-- The same failure as R2.30's MSQUASH, in a different constant: a transition guard
	-- whose transition no longer happens, left standing as a permanent state. A ramp that
	-- nothing ever walks up is not a ramp, it is a dimmer on one mark.
	--
	-- Marks DO still cross the cut-off, which is why a ramp survives at all -- a mark 3 m
	-- along the path is nearer than 3 m in a straight line when the path bends, the route
	-- ends, and the spacing changes under MCM. Those are transitions, so the ramp is now
	-- sized for transitions: 0.6 m, clearing 3.0 m, below every gap's first parking spot.
	-- Revert = 3.0 / 2.0.
	MNEAR = 2.4,   -- metres: below this a mark is not drawn at all
	MFADE = 0.6,   -- metres over which it ramps back to full above MNEAR

	-- THE PULSE'S SHAPE (R2.43). Amplitude is the MCM option (route_pulse); these two are
	-- feel, not preference, so they live here with the rest of the look.
	--
	-- PULSE_HZ is radians per second, so 2.4 is a period of about 2.6 s -- slow enough to
	-- read as a swell rather than a flicker, and deliberately NOT near the conveyor's own
	-- rate: two periodic motions at similar rates beat against each other, which reads as
	-- the route stuttering rather than as either effect.
	--
	-- PULSE_LAG is the phase step between one mark and the next, in radians. 0.9 puts a
	-- little under a full wavelength across the seven-ish marks of a default draw, so at
	-- any instant the route carries one visible crest travelling along it. Larger and
	-- neighbours disagree so much it reads as noise; at 0 every mark brightens together,
	-- which is the whole HUD flashing and is what this shape exists to avoid.
	PULSE_HZ  = 2.4,
	PULSE_LAG = 0.9,

	-- THE ALONG AXIS'S FLOOR, IN PIXELS (R2.30, replacing MSQUASH).
	--
	-- A mark lies on the floor, so its along-travel axis foreshortens with the grazing
	-- angle while its across axis stays broadside: measured at 55 degrees FOV, the true
	-- aspect runs 0.28 at 3 m to 0.023 at 40 m against an artwork aspect of 0.598. That
	-- 12x is not an error to be corrected -- it is the only thing that says the mark is
	-- PAINTED ON THE GROUND rather than standing up out of it, and it is the whole
	-- difference between a decal and a billboard.
	--
	-- R2.27 floored it as a FRACTION of the artwork's aspect (MSQUASH 0.4), to stop the
	-- far marks flattening into lines. Measure what that actually did and the floor is
	-- active at EVERY distance past 3.5 m -- so the entire visible run was drawn at one
	-- fixed aspect of 0.2392, i.e. every mark held the shape it has when seen from a
	-- fixed steep angle no matter where the ground it sits on really is. At 8 m that is
	-- twice the depth the ground has. The comment there predicted the failure ("past ~0.6
	-- they start to look like billboards standing up out of it") without noticing 0.4 had
	-- already reached it: a floor that always binds is not a floor, it is the design.
	-- Reported in the twenty-first session as the marks pointing UPWARDS instead of lying
	-- along the ground toward the target.
	--
	-- What the two-quad styles really had was seg_width's WMIN -- 2.5 PIXELS, an absolute
	-- legibility guard on the foreshortened quantity, which binds only once the thing is
	-- genuinely about to vanish. That is the right shape of clamp and R2.20's own rule
	-- (a clamp belongs to the quantity it was tuned against) asks for it in the same
	-- units. So: the honest projection everywhere it is readable, and a hard px floor at
	-- the tail where it is not. 6 px binds past ~15 m, where the mark is already under a
	-- tenth of its own width deep; below that nothing is touched.
	MLMIN = 6,
	-- (Chevrons take the same HEAD/HMIN fade as the stroke, measured from the same nearest
	-- drawn vertex. R2.19 gave them an absolute reference and R2.21 made it fade to zero,
	-- both to hold back a near GLYPH -- a problem the two-quad arms do not have, since each
	-- arm is a thin strip landed on its own two projected points. Reverted with the rest of
	-- R2.19-R2.21 in R2.22.)
	-- UI px of face-on width per metre of ground, at 1 m from the camera. Only used
	-- as a fallback for a segment whose ground edges will not project (the one
	-- straddling the near plane); re-derived from the first segment each frame that
	-- does project, so it tracks the player's FOV and resolution without asking for
	-- either. The initial value is a 768-high UI space at a ~55 degree vertical FOV.
	PPM   = 730,
	FADE  = 0.72,  -- fraction of the run after which it starts fading out
	TAIL  = 0.30,  -- alpha multiplier the far end fades to

	-- Two more scratch points, for the ends of a chevron arm. Plain tables rather than
	-- vectors on purpose: seg_width only reads .x/.y/.z off them, and a table costs
	-- nothing to keep and nothing to write.
	t1 = { x = 0, y = 0, z = 0 },
	t2 = { x = 0, y = 0, z = 0 },
}

-- Route sprite pools. iqm_nav caps itself at 20 drawn points with a chevron every
-- third, so these are its ceiling and not a second opinion about it; the beads pool
-- also has to cover the beads-only style, where every point is one.
-- One arrow per route mark. iqm_nav caps itself at MAX_DRAW of them, so this is its
-- ceiling rather than a second opinion about it: 50 m drawn at the tightest spacing
-- the menu offers (2 m) is 26 arrows.
local UI_KX       = 1     -- aspect-correction the engine applies to rotated (heading) statics
local _ui_w       = 0     -- last screen width UI_KX was computed for (recompute only on change)
local LINE_HALO   = 2     -- px added to the leader line thickness for its dark outline

-- Hot math globals localised (per-frame projection/draw path) to avoid repeated global
-- table lookups. math.log only runs one-time (read_config) so it's left as-is.
local floor, max, min, sqrt = math.floor, math.max, math.min, math.sqrt
local sin, abs, atan2 = math.sin, math.abs, math.atan2
-- cos joins them for place_mark's circular mean of the two angle candidates (R2.28).
local cos = math.cos
-- Other hot globals on the per-frame path, localised for the same reason.
local GetARGB, time_global = GetARGB, time_global
-- The projection itself, bound once (R2.50, docs/route-perf.md finding 4.3). It was reached
-- as `game.world2ui_with_depth` inside project_off, i.e. a global lookup plus a field lookup
-- on EVERY projection -- the hottest line in this file, ~210 lookups a frame on a default
-- route and shared with iqm_beacon's markers through project_offscreen below.
--
-- BOUND ON FIRST USE, in project_off below. This took three goes and the first two each
-- shipped a real defect, so the reasoning is worth keeping.
--
-- (1) EAGERLY, at file scope: `local world2ui = game.world2ui_with_depth`. Rejected because
--     nothing in the modpack relies on `game` existing at load -- a grep of base Anomaly's
--     whole script tree finds NOT ONE file-scope binding of a `game.*` member -- and the cost
--     of being wrong is the worst failure this file has: indexing a nil `game` at load is a
--     parse failure that takes the cards, the beacons, the route and the readout down
--     together. It also broke marks-harness at once, whose env is deliberately the thinnest
--     thing that lets this file load.
--
-- (2) ASSIGNED IN apply_config, on the reasoning that iqm_core calls it at the end of
--     read_config (iqm_core:963) inside on_game_start, before actor_on_update is even
--     registered -- so nothing could draw first. On a fresh start that holds. IT CRASHED ON A
--     SAVE LOAD anyway, and shipped in R2.50:
--
--         iqm_cards.script:503: attempt to call upvalue 'world2ui' (a nil value)
--         ... from draw_beacons
--
--     Loading a save re-reads the .script files, so this upvalue is nil again in the fresh
--     chunk, and on that path a marker is drawn before apply_config has run. The exact
--     mechanism is NOT established -- it wants one more load with a probe here -- and that is
--     the lesson: an assumption about engine lifecycle that read as obviously true, survived
--     review, passed twenty harnesses, and was false.
--
-- (3) THIS. One branch on an already-hot function, the whole saving kept (an upvalue read per
--     projection instead of a global plus a field lookup), and NO load-order assumption at
--     all. It cannot be wrong about when the engine is ready, because it asks.
--
-- The harness lesson is recorded beside the guard in tools/route-render-harness: proving the
-- module LOADS is not proving a function can be CALLED before configuration. Nothing had ever
-- called project_off before apply_config, which is exactly the crash. Now something does.
local world2ui

-- The dialog itself. ONE of them, built lazily by ensure() below; iqm_core caches
-- what that returns so its render path does not resolve this through the namespace
-- eight times a frame.
local CARDS = nil

-- Mirrors the MCM debug_log option, refreshed in apply_config -- the same mirror
-- iqm_core keeps, because draw_slot's card-geometry dump came here with draw_slot
-- and a bare `DEBUG` would otherwise have resolved to a namespace nil: never true, no
-- error, and the log line simply gone. _dbg_t is that dump's own once-a-second gate.
local DEBUG  = false
local _dbg_t = 0
-- Reusable scratch vector2s: SetWndPos/SetWndSize consume their argument immediately, so
-- one pair per purpose can be :set() and handed over instead of allocating a fresh vector2
-- on every call (draw_slot previously did ~14 allocations per card per frame).
local _p = vector2()
local _s = vector2()

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- INTERFERENCE (R2.59)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- iqm_noise decides HOW HARD the Zone is leaning on the overlay; this is where that
-- becomes pixels, because this file owns the drawing. Everything it needs is on one
-- published table, bound in apply_config below and mutated by nobody here.
--
-- IT ALL HANGS OFF P(). Every world-space position in the whole mod goes through that one
-- function -- 24 sites in this file, and none anywhere else that draws in the world -- so a
-- displacement written there reaches the nameplates, the markers, the leader lines, the
-- drop shadows and the route's marks at once, with no site needing to know about it. Two
-- properties fall out of that and both are wanted:
--
--   * THE OFFSET CAN BE A FUNCTION OF THE ROW, because every widget hands P() its own y.
--     A displacement keyed on y is a horizontal band tear -- which is the analogue-video
--     signature the whole feature is imitating -- and it needs to know nothing about what
--     it is placing.
--   * SHADOWS TEAR WITH THEIR OWNERS, because the shadow offsets go through P() too. Had
--     they been positioned any other way, a torn card would shed its own drop shadow,
--     which reads as broken rather than as interfered with.
--
-- `NZ` starts as a REST TABLE and not nil, so P() never has to test for one: a nil index
-- on this path would not misdraw a card, it would take every widget in the mod down on the
-- same frame. apply_config rebinds it to the real published state, once.
local NZ = { amp = 0, fr = 0, B = {} }
-- Band count and 1/height, bound from iqm_noise.band_geom() in apply_config. Not restated:
-- an index built from the wrong height reads the wrong band or past the end of N.B. The
-- fallbacks are only so the first frame before any apply_config cannot divide by nil, and
-- with amp 0 they are never reached.
local NZ_NB, NZ_INV_BH = 9, 1 / 71
-- The fringe hue, bound the same way (iqm_noise owns what colour a chromatic fringe is).
local FR_R, FR_G, FR_B = 90, 210, 225

--- Scratch position, tear applied. The hot path in the whole feature: this runs once per
--  widget per frame, and with interference at rest it is one compare and the :set() that
--  was always there.
local function P(x, y)
	local a = NZ.amp
	if a == 0 then return _p:set(x, y) end
	-- floor() can go negative on an off-screen row; Lua's % is non-negative for a positive
	-- divisor, so the band index stays in range without a clamp.
	-- UI_KX because ui x-space is STRETCHED by it: an amplitude that skips the correction
	-- tears wider on an ultrawide than on 16:9 for the same setting.
	return _p:set(x + NZ.B[floor(y * NZ_INV_BH) % NZ_NB + 1] * a * UI_KX, y)
end

--- ...and the DARK COPY's position, which leans the other way.
--
--  This is the chromatic split, and it is the one interference effect that normally needs
--  a compositing pass we do not have. What makes it possible is that every text, every
--  leader line and every plate ALREADY draws a black copy of itself behind the ink. Offset
--  that copy against the tear and retint it (see SHC) and the fringe comes out of widgets
--  that are already created, already positioned and already costing a draw. We get one
--  side of the split; the glyph's own ink is the other.
--
--  Only the INK's copies use this -- the text shadows and the leader line. The soft drop
--  shadows under the card and the badge stay on P() and stay black: they are a big diffuse
--  slab standing for lift, and a cyan one is a coloured glow rather than a fringe.
local function PS(x, y)
	local a = NZ.amp
	if a == 0 then return _p:set(x, y) end
	local b = NZ.B[floor(y * NZ_INV_BH) % NZ_NB + 1]
	-- Displaced with the band like everything else, then pulled back along the tear by the
	-- fringe width -- so the separation is always across the direction of the tear, which
	-- is where a real one appears. Below T_CHROMA, fr is 0 and this is exactly P().
	return _p:set(x + (b * a - ((b >= 0) and NZ.fr or -NZ.fr)) * UI_KX, y)
end

--- A dark copy's colour: black, or the fringe hue once the ink has separated. One place, so
--  the four sites that tint a dark copy cannot drift apart on which colour that is.
local function SHC(a)
	if NZ.fr == 0 then return GetARGB(a, 0, 0, 0) end
	return GetARGB(a, FR_R, FR_G, FR_B)
end

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Corruption: the wrong letter, and the wrong glyph
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- LOOKALIKES, not random letters. A random substitution reads as noise; a lookalike reads
-- as a MISREAD -- the overlay trying to render the right character and getting it slightly
-- wrong, which is the fiction. It also nearly solves the width problem for free (see the
-- note at the cache key in draw_slot): O and 0, S and 5, I and 1 are within a pixel of each
-- other in any UI font, so a substituted header measures almost exactly as wide as the
-- clean one.
--
-- UPPERCASE ONLY, because only the HEADER is corrupted and every header is uppercase.
--
-- THE HEADER AND NOT THE NPC'S NAME, which is the decision worth defending. A wrong letter
-- in "REPORT BACK" is unmistakably corruption: the string is short, patterned, and one the
-- player has read a hundred times. A wrong letter in a surname they have never met is
-- INDISTINGUISHABLE from the real name -- so it is not an effect, it is just wrong data, and
-- the player has no way to tell the overlay is glitching rather than describing a different
-- person. The name is left alone for the same reason the give-up tier has a floor.
local LOOKALIKE = {
	O = "0", I = "1", S = "5", E = "3", A = "4", B = "8", G = "6", T = "7", Z = "2",
	L = "1", C = "(",
}

--- `str` with one character replaced, chosen from `seed`. Returns `str` itself when nothing
--  in it has a lookalike, which is the honest answer rather than a forced substitution.
--
--  Walks forward from the seeded position so the character that moves changes between beats
--  instead of one letter flickering in place, and so a header whose seeded slot happens to
--  be a space still corrupts somewhere.
--
--  Allocates two substrings and a concat. That is fine HERE and would not be on the frame
--  path: this runs only when the corruption key changes (a few times a second per card, and
--  only under psi pressure), on the same cache boundary that already pays a SetText.
local function corrupt_text(str, seed)
	local n = #str
	if n == 0 then return str end
	for k = 0, n - 1 do
		local i = ((seed + k) % n) + 1
		local sub = LOOKALIKE[str:sub(i, i)]
		if sub then return str:sub(1, i - 1) .. sub .. str:sub(i + 1) end
	end
	return str
end

-- The two glyph pools a corrupted icon is drawn from, built in apply_config from the tables
-- that OWN them -- iqm_beacon's role glyphs and iqm_core's faction emblems -- rather than
-- restated here. Restating twelve emblem strings is precisely the R2.56 mirror failure: two
-- lists with one meaning and no eye on the slower half.
local ROLE_POOL, FACT_POOL = {}, {}

--- A different texture from `tex`'s own family, chosen from `seed`. nil when there is no
--  pool to pick from or nothing else in it.
--
--  WITHIN THE FAMILY, and that is not tidiness. A role glyph is white-on-transparent and is
--  tinted to the accent colour; a faction emblem carries its own colours and is drawn
--  untinted (see the icon_tinted branch below). Swapping across the two would flip the
--  tinting mid-flicker and read as a colour bug rather than as a wrong glyph.
local function corrupt_icon(tex, seed, tinted)
	local pool = (tinted == false) and FACT_POOL or ROLE_POOL
	local n = #pool
	if n < 2 then return nil end
	-- Walk until it differs, so a roll that lands on the correct glyph is not a wasted beat
	-- that silently draws nothing wrong.
	for k = 0, n - 1 do
		local pick = pool[((seed + k) % n) + 1]
		if pick ~= tex then return pick end
	end
	return nil
end

local function S(x, y) return _s:set(x, y) end   -- scratch size

-- Beacon projection. Unlike project_world this ASKS for off-screen coordinates
-- (allow_offscreen = true), because a beacon that is off-screen still has to be
-- placed -- clamped to the screen edge, pointing the way. The third component is
-- a SIGN, not a distance: world2ui_with_depth returns -1 when the point is behind
-- the camera (level_script.cpp:1691), where the perspective divide by a negative w
-- mirrors x/y through the screen centre. Callers must undo that (see draw_beacons).
local _b2u = vector()
local function project_off(x, y, z)
	-- Bind on first use (see the note on world2ui). Nil-safe both ways: if `game` is not up
	-- yet this returns nil, which every caller already handles as "did not project", rather
	-- than erroring -- and the binding is retried on the next call instead of being poisoned.
	local w = world2ui
	if not w then
		w = game and game.world2ui_with_depth
		if not w then return nil end
		world2ui = w
	end
	local ui = w(_b2u:set(x, y, z), false, true)
	if not ui then return nil end
	return ui.x, ui.y, ui.z
end
-- Published for iqm_beacon, which places its markers in this same projected space.
-- It stays a file LOCAL as well, because the route projects with it several times per
-- vertex per frame and that read must not become a hash lookup -- so this is the same
-- function under a second, public name.
--
-- The name has to DIFFER. `project_off = project_off` looks like the obvious spelling
-- and is a silent no-op: the local is in scope from its own declaration onward, so
-- both sides resolve to it and nothing is ever written to the namespace. iqm_beacon
-- would then bind nil and every marker would die on the first frame one was drawn --
-- which no harness runs, so nothing would have caught it.
project_offscreen = project_off

-- The selected shape's bounding box, into RTE.SB. Computed on config change, never per
-- mark: it depends only on which shape is chosen.
--
-- MIRRORS shape_box() in tools/stroke-tex/build.py, which is what drew the picture into
-- that box; a disagreement shows as the glyph sitting off its own rect. The chevron's is
-- the only asymmetric one, because it alone has a mitre past the tip: an arm runs from
-- the tip (hl, 0) to a back corner (-hl, +/-hw) and is a rectangle half a thickness
-- either side of that line, so the shape reaches `ha` further along the perpendicular
-- n = (hw, 2*hl)/L at the back and sides, and the two front edges meet ha*L/hw past the
-- tip. L is the arm's true length, hypot(2*hl, hw) -- getting that wrong is what clipped
-- the point off the artwork until R2.26. The arrow is a convex box with no mitre.
-- A module function rather than a file local, and it takes an INDEX and RETURNS the box
-- it just stored. Neither is for the renderer's benefit -- read_config calls it bare and
-- reads RTE.SB. Both are so a harness can call it: the first version of this was a local
-- that took no arguments and returned nothing, which made it untestable, and it shipped
-- with `sqrt` resolving to a global nil because it sat above the file's math locals. It
-- compiled perfectly and killed the game on load (R2.32a). A thing no test can call is a
-- thing no test will catch.
function shape_box(idx)
	-- RTE.SHAPES has HOLES in it (the retired numbers), so an index that is in range is
	-- not necessarily a shape. A plain lookup with a fallback covers both, which is why
	-- there is no bounds test here.
	local s = RTE.SHAPES[idx or RTE.shape or 7] or RTE.SHAPES[7]
	-- A shape may carry its own width and stroke weight (shape 7 does); everything else
	-- takes the family's. Mirrored in tools/stroke-tex/build.py's shape_box, including
	-- that the PAD scales with the arm -- the feather and the baked shadow are both
	-- fractions of the stroke weight, so a thinner-armed shape gets a proportionally
	-- smaller margin and the box stays exactly similar to the picture drawn into it.
	local hl, hw = s.alen * 0.5, (s.awide or RTE.awide) * 0.5
	local aw  = s.aw or RTE.aw
	local ha  = aw * 0.5
	-- The feather's margin plus the baked shadow's reach: both are room the artwork uses
	-- outside the glyph's own outline, and both have to be in the box or what sits in
	-- them is cut off at the rect's edge.
	local pad = (RTE.FPAD + RTE.SPAD) * aw
	if s.kind == "chevron" then
		local L = sqrt(4 * hl * hl + hw * hw)
		RTE.SB.back = hl + ha * hw / L + pad
		RTE.SB.fwd  = hl + ha * L / hw + pad
		RTE.SB.half = hw + ha * 2 * hl / L + pad
	else   -- arrow: spans +/-hl along travel and is widest at the back
		RTE.SB.back, RTE.SB.fwd, RTE.SB.half = hl + pad, hl + pad, hw + pad
	end
	return RTE.SB.back, RTE.SB.fwd, RTE.SB.half
end

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The card dialog: a fixed pool of card widget-sets, positioned per frame.
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
class "IqmCards" (CUIScriptWnd)

function IqmCards:__init() super()
	self:InitControls()
end

function IqmCards:InitControls()
	self:SetWndRect(Frect():set(0, 0, 1024, 768))
	self:SetAutoDelete(true)

	self.xml = CScriptXmlInit()
	self.xml:ParseFile("iqm_cards.xml")

	-- The route, created BEFORE the cards and beacons so it draws underneath both: the
	-- route is ground furniture, and a mark landing over a card's text would be the one
	-- thing on screen you cannot read around.
	--
	-- ONE pool and ONE widget per mark since R2.26c. There used to be four pools -- stroke
	-- segments, chevron arms, glyphs and billboard chevrons -- because there were five
	-- route styles and a widget's texture is fixed when it is created, so each design
	-- needed its own. Only one pool was ever in use in a frame; the rest sat parked. With
	-- the other styles dropped they are simply gone, and with the keyline baked into
	-- iqm_mark (R2.26b) the backing copy each mark used to carry is gone with them: 16
	-- statics where there were 152.
	self.rmarks = {}
	for i = 1, RTE.MARKS do
		self.rmarks[i] = self.xml:InitStatic("route_mark", self)
		self.rmarks[i]:Show(false)
		-- HEADING IS ENABLED ONCE, HERE (R2.50, docs/route-perf.md finding 4.2). It is a
		-- sticky widget flag, and place_mark used to re-assert it on every mark on every
		-- frame -- ~960 engine calls a second for a bool that never changes. This is the
		-- same move ea3263b made for the minimap trail's marks; the route's copy was
		-- missed then because no harness drew a frame through this file.
		--
		-- It has to be re-asserted after any InitTexture, in case re-pointing a texture
		-- resets it: see apply_shape and place_mark's variant branch, which are the only
		-- two places that do that. Both fire about once a second per slot at worst,
		-- rather than sixty times.
		self.rmarks[i]:EnableHeading(true)
	end
	-- The xml gives them the default shape's cell; read_config has already chosen which
	-- shape is wanted, and it could not point the pool at it because the pool did not
	-- exist yet. This is that second half.
	self:apply_shape()
	self.rg_used = 0

	self.slots = {}
	for i = 1, MAX_CARDS do
		-- Creation order = draw order (later draws on top). Back to front:
		--  * card_shadow      -- soft slab under the whole card (physical lift)
		--  * line_sh/line     -- leader line, BEFORE the plate so the plate draws over its
		--                        end (the line is extended slightly into the card => flush join)
		--  * plate / chip     -- rounded gradient plate: one quad each (text card / icon chip)
		--  * accent           -- left edge bar
		--  * icon             -- role glyph (ambient cards only; texture set at draw time)
		--  * *_sh / text      -- header/name, each with a 1px black shadow copy behind it.
		--                        Parented to `self` (not the plate) and created AFTER the
		--                        plate, so the text draws on top of it.
		--  * glow/node        -- head dot + its soft halo (drawn last; they sit on the NPC's
		--                        body, away from the card, so top-of-stack is fine)
		local shadow  = self.xml:InitStatic("card_shadow", self)
		local line_sh = self.xml:InitStatic("card_line_sh", self)
		local line    = self.xml:InitStatic("card_line", self)
		local plate   = self.xml:InitStatic("card_plate", self)
		local chipbg  = self.xml:InitStatic("card_chip", self)
		local accent  = self.xml:InitStatic("card_accent", self)
		local icon    = self.xml:InitStatic("card_icon", self)
		local head_sh = self.xml:InitTextWnd("card_head_sh:text", self)
		local name_sh = self.xml:InitTextWnd("card_name_sh:text", self)
		local head    = self.xml:InitTextWnd("card_head:text", self)
		local name    = self.xml:InitTextWnd("card_name:text", self)
		local glow    = self.xml:InitStatic("card_glow", self)
		local node    = self.xml:InitStatic("card_node", self)
		self.slots[i] = { shadow = shadow, plate = plate, chip = chipbg, accent = accent,
		                  icon = icon, line_sh = line_sh, line = line,
		                  glow = glow, node = node,
		                  head = head, name = name, head_sh = head_sh, name_sh = name_sh }
		self:hide_slot(i)
	end

	-- Hand-in beacon widget sets, created AFTER the cards so they draw on top: a
	-- beacon means "you can't see this objective right now", which shouldn't end up
	-- tucked behind somebody else's card.
	self.beacons = {}
	for i = 1, MAX_BEACONS do
		-- back to front: shadow, plate, chevron, then the badge contents. No dark-copy
		-- widgets: every glyph here carries its keyline in the art (R2.31), which is
		-- both why they cannot drift out of alignment and why this list is short.
		local bshad = self.xml:InitStatic("beacon_shadow", self)
		local plate = self.xml:InitStatic("beacon_plate", self)
		local chev  = self.xml:InitStatic("beacon_chevron", self)
		-- the waypoint ring goes UNDER the glyph: it frames the role mark, it does not
		-- compete with it
		local ring  = self.xml:InitStatic("beacon_ring", self)
		local icon  = self.xml:InitStatic("beacon_icon", self)
		-- The range readout: one widget per character, MAX_RGLYPH of them (three digits
		-- -- beacon_dist stops at 200 -- plus the metre suffix). Created up front and
		-- reused, because a widget's texture is settable but a widget is not creatable
		-- on the draw path.
		local gl = {}
		for k = 1, MAX_RGLYPH do
			gl[k] = { g = self.xml:InitStatic("beacon_digit", self), tex = nil }
		end
		-- ...and the name, last so it draws over the rest. Shadow first, as the cards do.
		local nm_sh = self.xml:InitTextWnd("beacon_name_sh:text", self)
		local nm    = self.xml:InitTextWnd("beacon_name:text", self)
		-- `gs` is the park loops' high-water mark: how many of the four glyph widgets the
		-- last frame left VISIBLE (route-perf 4.7). Seeded at MAX_RGLYPH rather than 0 so
		-- the hide_beacon below parks the whole pool once -- InitStatic hands back a
		-- visible widget, which is the reason these init-time hide calls exist at all.
		self.beacons[i] = { shadow = bshad, plate = plate, icon = icon, ring = ring,
		                    chev = chev, gl = gl, gn = 0, gs = MAX_RGLYPH, tex = nil,
		                    -- ...and the RING's texture cache beside the glyph's (R2.63).
		                    -- Not cleared by invalidate_beacons, and neither is `tex`: both
		                    -- are keyed on the texture WANTED, so a stale entry can only
		                    -- ever be a correct one. (apply_shape's rmvar has to be cleared
		                    -- because it is keyed on a variant INDEX, which means something
		                    -- different after a shape change -- a different kind of cache.)
		                    rtex = nil,
		                    nm = nm, nm_sh = nm_sh, nmt = nil, nmw = 0 }
		self:hide_beacon(i)
	end
end

-- Drop the cached readouts and park every marker. Needed on a config change because
-- each widget set only rebuilds its character list when the NUMBER changes, so a
-- readout laid out for the old glyph size would otherwise persist until the player
-- happened to walk a metre.
function IqmCards:invalidate_beacons()
	for i = 1, MAX_BEACONS do
		local b = self.beacons and self.beacons[i]
		if b then b.m = nil end
		self:hide_beacon(i)
	end
	self.bhigh = 0   -- everything is down: draw_beacons' high-water mark starts over
end

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The route: one continuous stroke along the path to the objective NPC
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Constants, style and scratch all live in RTE, near the other config mirrors.
function IqmCards:hide_route()
	for i = 1, self.rg_used or 0 do
		self.rmarks[i]:Show(false)
	end
	self.rg_used = 0
end

--- The on-screen thickness of one stroke segment, in UI px.
--
--  MEASURED, not computed (R2.13). The old formula was `route_w * REF / d`, which is
--  the apparent size of something FACING the camera. A stroke lying on the ground is
--  not facing it: its width runs along the ground plane, so it is foreshortened by the
--  grazing angle, and by how much depends on which way the path runs. A stretch
--  crossing left-to-right in front of you is much thinner than one running away at the
--  same distance, and a single scalar over `d` cannot say that at any tuning -- which
--  is why the near half of the stroke used to be a constant-width bar (`WMAX` clamping
--  a value that was wrong anyway) rather than a band converging into the distance.
--
--  So: the two edges of the ribbon are real world positions -- the segment midpoint
--  plus and minus the ground-plane perpendicular times half the width -- and projecting
--  THOSE gives the thickness the geometry actually has. Every perspective cue falls out
--  of it at once and correct: convergence with range, the grazing-angle squeeze, and
--  the widening of a path cresting a rise toward you. Two projections per segment.
--
--  Only the component ACROSS the segment counts. Under perspective the edge-to-edge
--  vector is skewed along the segment as well, and that part is not thickness; taking
--  the raw separation would fatten every oblique stretch.
--  `cap` overrides the ceiling. It exists because the ceiling is a SANITY limit expressed
--  in px, and px is only meaningful against the width being measured: at RTE.WMAX = 96 it
--  is several times the widest stroke the menu offers, and about a third of what a chevron
--  glyph legitimately measures at two metres (the glyph's box is 1.2 m across against the
--  band's 0.30). Clamping there crushed the glyph's HEIGHT while its length went on being
--  measured, and since a rect's aspect is what carries the mark's shape, the mark appeared
--  to change its angle with distance (R2.20).
function IqmCards:seg_width(pa, pb, ax, ay, bx, by, d, ww, cap)
	local th
	local dx, dz = pb.x - pa.x, pb.z - pa.z
	local m = sqrt(dx * dx + dz * dz)
	if m > 1e-4 then
		local hw = ww * 0.5
		local nx, nz = -dz / m * hw, dx / m * hw
		local mx, my, mz = (pa.x + pb.x) * 0.5, (pa.y + pb.y) * 0.5, (pa.z + pb.z) * 0.5
		local lx, ly, ls = project_off(mx + nx, my, mz + nz)
		local rx, ry, rs = project_off(mx - nx, my, mz - nz)
		if lx and rx and ls and rs and ls > 0 and rs > 0 then
			-- KX-corrected space throughout, the same one place_seg builds its rect in
			local sepx, sepy = (lx - rx) / UI_KX, ly - ry
			local ux, uy = (bx - ax) / UI_KX, by - ay
			local ul = sqrt(ux * ux + uy * uy)
			if ul > 1e-4 then
				th = abs(sepx * (-uy / ul) + sepy * (ux / ul))
			else
				th = sqrt(sepx * sepx + sepy * sepy)
			end
			-- Feed the fallback scale below. The high-water mark over the frame rather
			-- than the last value, because a grazing segment measures small and would
			-- drag the estimate down: what is wanted is the face-on scale, and any route
			-- with a stretch running away from you supplies it.
			if d > 0.5 then
				local ppm = th * d / ww
				if ppm > RTE.ppmf then RTE.ppmf = ppm end
			end
		end
	end
	-- Fallback: the segment straddling the near plane, whose edges are behind the
	-- camera. Face-on scale only -- the grazing term needs the projection we just
	-- failed to get -- which is honest enough for the one segment it applies to.
	if not th then th = ww * RTE.PPM / max(d, 0.5) end
	if th < RTE.WMIN then return RTE.WMIN end
	cap = cap or RTE.WMAX
	if th > cap then return cap end
	return th
end

--- Draw iqm_nav's published vertex list as one continuous stroke.
--
--  A segment is a solid quad stretched between two projected vertices and rotated
--  onto the line joining them -- exactly the leader line's trick, which has been
--  landing its ends on the head point and the card corner since the first version.
--  So this is not new geometry, it is the one piece of drawing this UI layer can do
--  that ISN'T a billboard, applied to the route.
--
--  Drawn FAR TO NEAR. Widget draw order is fixed at creation, so the only control we
--  have over which quad lands on top is which slot a segment gets: handing the
--  farthest one the lowest slot means nearer parts of the stroke overdraw farther
--  ones, which is the only ordering that looks right where the line doubles back on
--  itself round a corner.
--
--  Everything here is rotated, so nothing takes the UI_KX squeeze; see place_seg.
--- `n` / `nc` are how much of `rd` to draw, from iqm_nav.route_limit: the list is
--  published at the longer of the two views' distances, and this one may want less.
--  Both default to the whole list, which is what the debug drivers and the harness pass.
--
--  `am` is the SUMMON ENVELOPE (iqm_core, SMN) -- 0..1, eased there, defaulting to 1 so
--  every existing caller and every harness is unchanged. It multiplies the configured
--  opacity and nothing else, which is the whole reason the fade needed no new geometry:
--  the two-ended fade, the occlusion verdict and the dead-end ramp are already multipliers
--  on the same number, so the route fades out exactly as it is, mid-flow and mid-occlusion,
--  instead of being switched off.
function IqmCards:draw_route(rd, n, nc, am)
	n  = n  or rd.n
	nc = nc or rd.nc
	-- ONE device() call, two fields off it. cam_dir is new at R2.50: the head fade's reference
	-- distance is now found with a dot product against it rather than by projecting every
	-- vertex (see below). Reading both off one call keeps this at the single engine crossing
	-- it always was.
	local dev = device()
	local cam, cdir = dev.cam_pos, dev.cam_dir
	-- Hoisted out of RTE once per frame rather than looked up per segment. These
	-- are function locals, which have their own budget and do not touch the main
	-- chunk's 200 (see the note on RTE).
	-- The summon envelope folded into the hoisted opacity, so it costs one multiply per
	-- FRAME rather than one per segment and per chevron. nil or 1 leaves it untouched.
	local alpha = (am and am < 1) and (RTE.a * am) or RTE.a
	local cr, cg, cb = RTE.r, RTE.g, RTE.b
	-- The pulse's phase for THIS frame, hoisted: time_global() is stamped once per frame,
	-- so every mark shares one clock and the wave moves rigidly -- the same reason the
	-- conveyor uses it rather than time_global_async (iqm_nav.flow_phase).
	local pamp = RTE.pulse or 0
	local pt = (pamp > 0) and (time_global() * 0.001 * RTE.PULSE_HZ) or 0
	-- HOW MUCH ROOM THIS COLOUR HAS TO BRIGHTEN, as a multiplier, computed from the
	-- brightest channel: at 255/max the glyph is as bright as it can be drawn with its hue
	-- intact, and past that a channel pins and the colour drifts. So the amplitude is a
	-- FRACTION OF THAT HEADROOM rather than a fraction of the colour itself (R2.43a).
	--
	-- The first version multiplied the colour directly, and it was invisible in game --
	-- measured off the live widgets at route 45/95/50, where the crest reached 57/121/64:
	-- a lift of +12/+26/+14 out of 255, on a small glyph against sunlit concrete. The same
	-- setting on the olive default lifts +52/+58/+37, which is what it was tuned against.
	-- A multiply gives the LEAST absolute lift to the darkest colour, and a dark mark is
	-- already the one with the least contrast against the ground -- so the effect faded out
	-- exactly where it was needed most.
	--
	-- Headroom-relative fixes that without a special case: 1.0 means "as bright as this
	-- colour goes", whatever the colour is. Hue is exact at every amplitude, because it is
	-- still one multiplier across all three channels, and it can no longer clip.
	--
	-- The honest limit: a route colour already at 255 in some channel has no headroom and
	-- cannot pulse. That is unavoidable for a multiply and it is the right failure -- the
	-- alternative is drifting the hue on exactly the colours a player picked deliberately.
	local phead = 0
	if pamp > 0 then
		local mx = max(cr, max(cg, cb))
		phead = (mx > 0) and (255 / mx - 1) or 0
	end
	local FADE, TAIL = RTE.FADE, RTE.TAIL
	local MNEAR, MFADE = RTE.MNEAR, RTE.MFADE
	local HEAD, HMIN = RTE.HEAD, RTE.HMIN
	-- Per-frame high-water mark for the projection scale seg_width falls back on;
	-- applied to RTE.PPM at the end of the frame. See seg_width.
	RTE.ppmf = 0

	-- WHERE THE HEAD FADE MEASURES FROM: the camera distance of the nearest vertex in
	-- front of the camera. One number, and the only thing the vertex pass ever produced.
	--
	-- R2.50 (docs/route-perf.md finding 4.1). This used to be a full projection of every
	-- vertex into RTE.sx/sy/sd, a clip_front recovery pass over the near end, and then a
	-- min over the result -- 25-41 engine calls a frame at 0.387 us and 96 BYTES each,
	-- about 236 KB/s of garbage, to compute one scalar. The projection was left over from
	-- the stroke: sy was written and never read, sx was only ever nil-tested, and sd fed
	-- nothing but this minimum. The stroke went at R2.26c and its scaffolding stayed.
	--
	-- WHY A DOT PRODUCT IS THE SAME ANSWER. project_off with allow_offscreen never fails
	-- for a point in front of the camera, so the old `x and s and s > 0` test was asking
	-- only "is this in front" -- which is the sign of (p - cam) . cam_dir. The distance was
	-- already computed here by hand, not read out of the projection.
	--
	-- AND WHY LOSING clip_front DOES NOT MOVE d0. That pass gave a vertex behind the near
	-- plane a BORROWED depth, sd[i] = max(sd[i+1], 0.5), where i+1 had itself projected --
	-- so the value it contributed was never smaller than sd[i+1], which was already in the
	-- minimum. Dropping the borrow therefore cannot change the result. (Proved rather than
	-- assumed: tools/route-render-harness case 4c walks the near vertices behind the camera
	-- and pins the resulting alpha, and it did not move when this landed.)
	--
	-- The plane through cam_pos is a few centimetres from the true near plane; against a
	-- 1.5 m HEAD ramp that is under 1% of one mark's alpha at the very near end.
	local fx, fy, fz = cdir.x, cdir.y, cdir.z
	local d0 = 1e9
	for i = 1, n do
		local p = rd.p[i]
		local dx, dy, dz = p.x - cam.x, p.y - cam.y, p.z - cam.z
		if dx * fx + dy * fy + dz * fz > 0 then
			local d = sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz)
			if d < d0 then d0 = d end
		end
	end
	if d0 > 1e8 then d0 = 0 end

	-- The stroke's width, and whether there is a stroke at all. In the dense styles a
	-- chevron every few metres carries the path and the line is only there to join them
	-- up, so it is narrower; in `arrows` it is gone entirely and the marks ARE the route.
	-- NO STROKE, and no style to ask about (R2.26c). The four other route designs were
	-- dropped after the nineteenth session: the two-quad chevron cannot take a bold arm
	-- without opening a notch at its point, the billboard chevron stands up out of the
	-- path, and a stroke under marks this size is a band with a band on it. What is left
	-- is the design that survived being looked at.
	-- Chevrons. iqm_nav hands them over as their own list -- world position, unit
	-- direction of travel, and the vertex whose eased alpha each borrows -- rather than as
	-- flags on the vertices, so their spacing is exact metres of path (R2.16).
	--
	-- ONE rendering: the one-piece glyph lying flat on the floor (place_mark). The
	-- billboard alternative is gone with the other styles -- a mark lying on the ground at
	-- 12 m is foreshortened about 7:1 by the grazing angle and a billboard draws it
	-- uncompressed, so it stood up out of the path, the same error R2.13 fixed for the
	-- stroke's own width.
	local nm = 0
	for k = nc, 1, -1 do                -- far to near, as the stroke is
		local cp = rd.cp[k]
		local am = rd.a[rd.ci[k] or 1] or 1
		if cp and am > 0.02 then
			-- IN FRONT OF THE CAMERA? A dot product, not a projection (R2.50, finding 4.1).
			-- This site projected the chevron's centre and then used nothing but the sign of
			-- the third component -- cx and cy were never read -- for another 16 engine calls
			-- and 16 allocations a frame at the default spacing. place_mark re-projects the
			-- mark's own two axes anyway and rejects it if either lands behind, so the test
			-- here is a cheap reject, not the authority.
			local dx, dy, dz = cp.x - cam.x, cp.y - cam.y, cp.z - cam.z
			if dx * fx + dy * fy + dz * fz > 0 then
				local cd = sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz)
				-- the same two-ended fade the segments take, so a chevron never survives
				-- at full strength on a stretch of stroke that has faded out under it
				local t  = (nc > 1) and ((k - 1) / nc) or 0
				local fade = 1
				if t > FADE then
					fade = 1 - (t - FADE) / (1 - FADE) * (1 - TAIL)
				else
					local near = ((cd or 1e9) - d0) / HEAD
					if near < 1 then fade = HMIN + (1 - HMIN) * max(0, near) end
				end
				-- The near cut-off (RTE.MNEAR): a mark this close cannot be drawn
				-- correctly by one quad, so it is not drawn. Ramped over MFADE rather
				-- than switched, because a world-anchored mark crosses this threshold as
				-- you walk and a hard edge there would pop.
				if cd < MNEAR then
					fade = 0
				elseif cd < MNEAR + MFADE then
					fade = fade * (cd - MNEAR) / MFADE
				end
				local a = floor(alpha * am * fade)
				if a > 255 then a = 255 end
				local mr, mg, mb = cr, cg, cb
				-- THE PULSE (R2.43). A travelling wave, phased off the mark's own stable
				-- id so the crest runs ALONG the route rather than every mark breathing
				-- together. Brightens only: the tint MULTIPLIES the artwork, so scaling it
				-- up drives the glyph toward full strength and reads as paint catching
				-- light, where modulating alpha would read as blinking -- a warning lamp
				-- rather than a glow.
				--
				-- sin is in [-1, 1] and the wave wants [0, 1]: a mark should sit at its
				-- configured colour and brighten FROM there, never darken below it.
				-- Halving and offsetting is what puts the trough exactly on the colour the
				-- player chose, so this stays an effect on the route rather than becoming
				-- a second colour setting nobody asked for.
				if pamp > 0 then
					local s = 0.5 + 0.5 * sin(pt - (rd.cn and rd.cn[k] or k) * RTE.PULSE_LAG)
					local g = 1 + pamp * phead * s
					mr, mg, mb = floor(cr * g), floor(cg * g), floor(cb * g)
					if mr > 255 then mr = 255 end
					if mg > 255 then mg = 255 end
					if mb > 255 then mb = 255 end
				end
				if a > 2 then
					nm = self:place_mark(nm, cp, rd.cdx[k], rd.cdz[k], cd, a, mr, mg, mb,
					                     rd.cn and rd.cn[k])
				end
			end
		end
	end

	-- Carry this frame's projection scale into the next one. Read a frame late on
	-- purpose: the near-plane segment that needs it is drawn LAST (far to near), so a
	-- value settled over the whole previous frame is both available and steadier than
	-- one built up mid-loop.
	if RTE.ppmf > 0 then RTE.PPM = RTE.ppmf end

	-- SHOW AND PARK, both bounded by what CHANGED (R2.50, finding 4.2). self.rg_used is still
	-- last frame's count at this point -- place_mark does not touch it -- so it is exactly the
	-- set of slots already on screen, and the two loops below are the difference in each
	-- direction. Both are empty on a steady route, which is the normal case and the whole
	-- point: the show loop replaces a Show(true) per mark per frame.
	--
	-- The park loop was already bounded by rg_used rather than by RTE.MARKS, which is why
	-- this file never had the high-water bug ea3263b found in iqm_minimap's hide_from.
	local prev = self.rg_used or 0
	for i = prev + 1, nm do self.rmarks[i]:Show(true) end
	for i = nm + 1, prev do self.rmarks[i]:Show(false) end
	self.rg_used = nm
end

--- One direction chevron LYING ON THE GROUND, as ONE textured quad (R2.20).
--
--  This is the answer to two sessions of apex trouble, and the reason it works is worth
--  stating exactly. A heading-rotated static is rotate-then-uniform-scale (see place_strip)
--  but its rect's width and height are set independently, and `stretch="1"` maps the
--  texture onto that rect. So a non-square rect holding a chevron picture is precisely
--  "squash the mark along its own axis by h/w, then rotate it" -- which is what
--  perspective does to a mark lying on the floor. The foreshortening comes out of the
--  rect and the SHAPE comes out of the artwork, where it can be mitred, tapered and
--  antialiased properly by a tool that has real drawing primitives.
--
--  And the apex problem simply stops existing. Two quads cannot meet at a point: overlap
--  them and the second alpha pass leaves a(2-a) rather than a, which against darker ground
--  is a light spot (R2.19); butt them and the wedge between their end cuts is a notch.
--  Inside the texture the two arms are unioned by max() before anything is blended.
--
--  What it costs is the shear. The true ground-to-screen map of a small patch is
--  R' . S . R -- rotate the patch onto the compression axis, squash, rotate onto the
--  screen -- and a rect gives R' . diag(w, h), i.e. it assumes the mark's own axis IS the
--  compression axis. That is exact when the route runs away from the camera or straight
--  across it, and wrong in between, where the mark should be a parallelogram and is drawn
--  as a rectangle. Walking a route means looking down it, which is the exact case, and
--  the worst case (a mark 45 degrees to the view) is a decoration a few metres wide.
--
--  The rect is MEASURED, not computed: project the box's back-centre and front-centre for
--  the length and heading, and hand the across-travel extent to seg_width, which is the
--  same ground-edge measurement the stroke's thickness uses. So the glyph foreshortens by
--  the same rule as everything else on the route, with no new tuning.
--
--  The box is not centred on the mark. It reaches further FORWARD than back, because it
--  has to hold the mitre -- the point of the chevron sits (aw/2)/tan(a) past the tip --
--  plus the feather margin the artwork leaves itself. mark_box() in the builder computes
--  the same two numbers; FPAD is the shared constant.
-- The active shape's bounding box, in mark metres, and the texture to draw it with.
--
-- MIRRORS shape_box() in tools/stroke-tex/build.py, which is what drew the picture into
-- that box. The chevron's is the only asymmetric one: an arm runs from the tip (hl, 0)
-- to a back corner (-hl, +/-hw) and is a rectangle half a thickness either side of that
-- line, so the shape reaches `ha` further along the perpendicular n = (hw, 2*hl)/L at the
-- back and sides, and the two front edges meet ha*L/hw past the tip -- the mitre. L is
-- the arm's true length, hypot(2*hl, hw); getting it wrong is what clipped the point off
-- the artwork until R2.26. The arrow is convex and its box is trivial.
--
-- Called from read_config only, so a shape switch costs one pass over 16 widgets and
-- nothing per frame.
-- A static's texture is fixed at creation, so the pool is re-pointed rather than rebuilt.
-- A pool per shape -- R2.26c's mistake in a new costume -- would leave the rest parked
-- every frame for a setting the player changes once. Called on config change and once at
-- init, since read_config runs before the dialog exists and cannot do this half itself.
function IqmCards:apply_shape()
	local s = RTE.SHAPES[RTE.shape] or RTE.SHAPES[7]
	if not self.rmarks then return end
	-- Every widget back to variant 1 (the bare id), and the per-slot cache cleared with
	-- them. Clearing it is the half that matters: the cache says "this widget already
	-- shows variant v", and after a shape change that is true of the OLD shape -- so a
	-- stale entry would suppress the re-point and leave the previous glyph on screen
	-- until that slot's variant happened to change.
	self.rmvar = self.rmvar or {}
	for i = 1, RTE.MARKS do
		local w = self.rmarks[i]
		-- EnableHeading rides along with the re-point, not because it is known to be
		-- cleared by InitTexture but because that is the only thing that plausibly could
		-- clear it, and the cost of being wrong in this direction is one engine call per
		-- shape change (see the note in InitControls). Being wrong the other way is every
		-- mark drawing unrotated, which is loud but would ship.
		if w then w:InitTexture(s.tex); w:EnableHeading(true) end
		self.rmvar[i] = 1
	end
end

function IqmCards:place_mark(nm, p, ux, uz, cd, a, cr, cg, cb, mid)
	if nm + 1 > RTE.MARKS then return nm end
	-- The glyph's BOX, in mark metres, and it must be the same box the builder drew into
	-- (shape_box() in tools/stroke-tex/build.py) or the picture lands off its own rect.
	-- Computed once per config change by shape_box below, not per mark: it depends only
	-- on which shape is selected.
	local back, fwd, half = RTE.SB.back, RTE.SB.fwd, RTE.SB.half
	local t1, t2 = RTE.t1, RTE.t2
	t1.x, t1.y, t1.z = p.x - ux * back, p.y, p.z - uz * back
	t2.x, t2.y, t2.z = p.x + ux * fwd, p.y, p.z + uz * fwd
	local ax, ay, as = project_off(t1.x, t1.y, t1.z)
	if not (ax and as and as > 0) then return nm end
	local bx, by, bs = project_off(t2.x, t2.y, t2.z)
	if not (bx and bs and bs > 0) then return nm end

	-- Across-travel extent of the box, measured exactly as a stroke segment's thickness
	-- is. The rect's height, and the h of the h/w squash above.
	--
	-- With its OWN ceiling, not the stroke's. The stroke's is 96 px, which a mark this wide
	-- passes legitimately at a couple of metres -- and clamping the height while the length
	-- goes on being measured distorts the aspect, which IS the mark's shape. RTE.MCAP is the
	-- UI space's own height: past that the mark is taller than the screen and something has
	-- gone wrong upstream.
	-- THE RECT IS THE MARK'S TWO PROJECTED AXES, each measured on its own (R2.28).
	--
	-- Not seg_width, which is a STROKE measurement: it takes only the component of the
	-- edge separation PERPENDICULAR to the segment's screen direction, because for a band
	-- the skew along its own length is not thickness. For a MARK that skew is not an error
	-- to project out -- it is the shape. And it made the height depend on the along axis,
	-- which is the badly conditioned one in the view the route is used in, so the mark's
	-- size inherited that axis's noise for no benefit.
	--
	-- A rotated rect's width axis sits at `ang` and its height axis at ang + 90 degrees.
	-- The angle below is built so the height axis lands on the ACROSS direction, so the
	-- height is that vector's own length and the width is the along vector's.
	local nx, nz = -uz, ux                 -- XZ perpendicular; (ux, uz) is unit
	local lx, ly, ls = project_off(p.x + nx * half, p.y, p.z + nz * half)
	local rx, ry, rs = project_off(p.x - nx * half, p.y, p.z - nz * half)
	local dxk, dy = (bx - ax) / UI_KX, by - ay
	local ln = sqrt(dxk * dxk + dy * dy)
	local th, cxk, cyy
	if lx and rx and ls and rs and ls > 0 and rs > 0 then
		cxk, cyy = (lx - rx) / UI_KX, ly - ry
		th = sqrt(cxk * cxk + cyy * cyy)
	else
		-- Both across points behind the camera: fall back to the stroke measurement,
		-- which needs only the two along points we already have.
		th = self:seg_width(t1, t2, ax, ay, bx, by, cd, half * 2, RTE.MCAP)
	end
	if th < RTE.WMIN then th = RTE.WMIN elseif th > RTE.MCAP then th = RTE.MCAP end
	if ln < 0.0001 and not cxk then return nm end

	-- THE ANGLE, from BOTH axes, weighted by how long each projects (R2.28).
	--
	-- Each axis gives a candidate: the along vector directly, and the across vector minus
	-- 90 degrees (in the y-up frame that negating dy puts us in, the across vector taken
	-- right-corner to left sits exactly +90 from the along one, so there is no sign to get
	-- wrong). Walking a route the along axis points away from you and projects to a few
	-- pixels, where a fraction of a pixel is degrees; looking across the route it is the
	-- across axis that collapses. Whichever is longer is the one to believe.
	--
	-- Combined as a WEIGHTED CIRCULAR MEAN rather than by picking one. Picking has a
	-- switch-over, and at the switch the two candidates differ by the keystone the affine
	-- fit cannot express -- so the mark would jump a degree or two exactly when the player
	-- crosses that geometry, which is one more thing changing as you move.
	local ang
	if cxk then
		local w1 = dxk * dxk + dy * dy
		local w2 = cxk * cxk + cyy * cyy
		local a1 = atan2(-dy, dxk)
		local a2 = atan2(-cyy, cxk) - 1.5707963
		-- Guard the degenerate case where the two disagree by more than a right angle
		-- (a near-zero along vector can land anywhere): trust the across one alone.
		if cos(a1 - a2) < 0 then w1 = 0 end
		ang = atan2(w1 * sin(a1) + w2 * sin(a2), w1 * cos(a1) + w2 * cos(a2))
	else
		ang = atan2(-dy, dxk)
	end

	-- The along axis's floor, in PIXELS (RTE.MLMIN) -- the same kind of guard WMIN is for
	-- a stroke's thickness, and for the same reason: it binds only where the quantity is
	-- about to vanish, and leaves the true projection alone everywhere else.
	if ln < RTE.MLMIN then ln = RTE.MLMIN end
	local mx, my = (ax + bx) * 0.5, (ay + by) * 0.5

	-- NO BACKING COPY (R2.26b). The keyline is in the artwork now -- see MARK_RIM in
	-- tools/stroke-tex/build.py -- and the widget that used to stand in for it is left
	-- parked.
	--
	-- It had to go rather than be tuned. A rect enlarged about its centre SCALES the
	-- picture inside it, and this glyph's box is deliberately asymmetric (it reaches
	-- further forward to hold the mitre), so the enlarged copy shifts along the mark's
	-- axis instead of surrounding it: the dark copy emerged on the leading edge only and
	-- hid behind the body everywhere else. That is a one-sided outline at any `edge`, and
	-- no value of EDGE/SHA makes a displaced copy concentric. R2.20 recorded the symptom
	-- as the outline reading "heavier at the point"; at this mark's size and sharpness it
	-- became a black bar across the top of every chevron.
	--
	-- The stroke and the two-quad arms keep theirs: place_strip's rect is symmetric about
	-- a strip that fills it, so there the scaled copy really does surround the body.
	nm = nm + 1
	local s = self.rmarks[nm]
	-- THE VARIANT (R2.42). `mid` is a stable id for this mark -- its lattice index while
	-- the conveyor runs, its slot otherwise -- so hashing it gives a picture that stays
	-- with the mark as it flows and looks unordered along the run.
	--
	-- Re-pointed only when it CHANGES, which is what keeps this off the per-frame cost
	-- sheet: a widget's texture is otherwise fixed at creation (see apply_shape), and at
	-- the default spacing a slot's variant changes about once a second while flowing and
	-- never at all when the marks are still. The cache is per slot because InitTexture is
	-- what it guards, and the widget is the thing that has a texture.
	if RTE.NVAR > 1 and mid then
		local h = (mid * 2654435761) % 4294967296
		local v = 1 + floor(h / 65536) % RTE.NVAR
		if self.rmvar[nm] ~= v then
			self.rmvar[nm] = v
			s:InitTexture(RTE.SHAPES[RTE.shape].tex .. RTE.VSUF[v])
			s:EnableHeading(true)      -- see InitControls: re-asserted after a re-point only
		end
	end
	-- WHAT IS ACTUALLY PER-FRAME (R2.50, docs/route-perf.md finding 4.2). Size, position and
	-- heading genuinely change every frame -- the mark rides the projection, and SetHeading is
	-- a heading-up rotation. EnableHeading moved to creation, and Show(true) moved to
	-- draw_route's tail, where it is issued only for slots that were NOT shown last frame:
	-- slots 1..nm are dense and nm is stable while a route is up, so re-showing them was ~960
	-- no-op engine calls a second, for ever.
	--
	-- SetTextureColor stays per frame. It is the one of the four that has a live input -- the
	-- pulse moves it every frame by design -- and caching it would need invalidation in
	-- hide_route, apply_shape and apply_config for a saving of ~0.1 us a mark. Left alone
	-- deliberately; route-perf.md 4.2 records it as the part not taken.
	s:SetWndSize(S(ln, th))
	s:SetWndPos(P(mx - ln * 0.5, my - th * 0.5))
	s:SetHeading(ang)
	s:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(a, cr, cg, cb))
	return nm
end

function IqmCards:hide_beacon(i)
	local b = self.beacons and self.beacons[i]
	if not b or b.hidden then return end   -- same short-circuit as hide_slot
	b.hidden = true
	b.shadow:Show(false)
	b.plate:Show(false)
	b.icon:Show(false)
	b.chev:Show(false)
	b.ring:Show(false)
	b.nm:Show(false)
	b.nm_sh:Show(false)
	for k = 1, b.gs or MAX_RGLYPH do b.gl[k].g:Show(false) end
	b.gs = 0
end

-- One beacon: a charcoal badge holding the role glyph over the range readout, with
-- the chevron hung off its edge pointing at the target.
--
-- (ax, ay) is where the chevron goes -- above the NPC's head, or the screen-edge
-- point when clamped -- and (ndx, ndy) is the UI-space unit direction it points.
-- The badge places ITSELF behind the chevron along that line, because only this
-- function knows how big the badge is: it is sized around the measured range
-- string, so the layout can't be done by the caller.
--
-- Two sizing rules here look contradictory and are both right:
--   * ROTATED (the chevron): no UI_KX squeeze. The engine multiplies a
--     heading-rotated element's X by UI_KX, and the 1024x768 virtual UI then
--     stretches X by w/1024 -- and UI_KX * w/1024 is exactly h/768, the Y factor.
--     The two combine to a UNIFORM scale, so a plain UI square renders as a screen
--     square that rotates rigidly. Squeezing first double-corrects, and the chevron
--     visibly fattens and thins as it sweeps the screen edge.
--   * UNROTATED (the glyph): squeeze as usual, like the node dot and card glyphs,
--     or the 4:3 virtual UI stretches it into an oval.
-- Badge metrics. Kept tight on purpose: the marker marks something you may not be
-- able to see, so it has to be legible, but it sits over live gameplay and is the
-- one element here with no natural size limit. Erring small and letting beacon_size
-- scale it up beats a marker that owns a corner of the screen.
local BADGE_PAD  = 4    -- px of plate around the content
local BADGE_GAP  = 1    -- px between the glyph and the readout
local BADGE_SHM  = 4    -- px the drop shadow extends past the plate
-- Draw the charcoal plate and its drop shadow behind the badge? False since R2.30, on
-- request. The layout still runs and the widgets still exist, so this is the one line
-- that brings the plate back -- see the note in iqm_cards.xml for why it was there.
local BADGE_PLATE = false
-- Name band (option beacon_name). NAME_H is the declared height of the text widget in
-- iqm_cards.xml and is used only to reserve the band -- the FONT's own pixel height is
-- the engine's business and is not knowable from here, which is the whole limitation.
local NAME_H   = 16
local NAME_GAP = 2      -- px between the name band and the badge
-- The waypoint ring's size as a multiple of the glyph. Two forces set it: big enough that
-- it frames rather than outlines (which is also what makes it safe as a separate widget --
-- see the note in iqm_cards.xml), small enough that the marker still reads as one mark
-- with a ring round it rather than as a ring with something in the middle. 1.5 was the
-- first cut and was reported as too big; the ring is also the widest thing on the badge at
-- that size, which made the whole marker grow when a waypoint was on it.
local RING_K   = 1.28
-- ...and the SELECTED TASK's ring, which cannot use it (R2.63a). Reported from play as
-- "the proportions are wrong", and the measurement says why in one number: the ring's
-- clear interior is 0.266 of its own width and the hand-in tag's ink reaches 0.430 of
-- its own, so the tag crosses the arcs at any K below 1.62. The WAYPOINT ring has exactly
-- the same problem and gets away with it because of where its GAPS are: the tag's widest
-- points sit at 15 and 75 degrees, inside that ring's +-20 degree cardinal gaps, so the
-- corners pass through the openings. The task ring's gaps are +-11 degrees (it mirrors
-- TASK_GAP_DEG, and that is not ours to widen), so the same corners land on solid arc.
--
-- So this ring buys its clearance instead, and buys it from BOTH sides rather than one:
-- the ring grows to 1.58 and the glyph inside it draws at 0.85. Either alone would have
-- done it (1.81 with the glyph untouched, or 0.70 with the badge unchanged -- which is
-- what ../map-icons does, RETICLE_FIT, because a map spot has a fixed cell and no third
-- option). Both were worse at the size this actually draws: 1.81 makes the selected mark
-- conspicuously bigger than its neighbours and goes past the 1.5 already reported as too
-- big, and 0.70 leaves a 14 px tag at the default marker size, where the mark stops being
-- a tag and becomes a ring with something in it. Split, the badge grows ~23% and the
-- glyph gives up 15%, and the arcs clear the tag's corners by about a pixel.
--
-- Keyed on the TEXTURE, which is what the candidate record carries and what both sides
-- already agree on (BEACON_RING in iqm_beacon). One failed hash lookup for an unringed
-- marker, which is nearly all of them, and the default below is the waypoint's.
local RING_GEOM = {
	["iqm_role_ringtask"] = { k = 1.58, fit = 0.85 },
}
-- `e` is the candidate record from beacon_offer, not a bag of loose arguments: it already
-- carries tex/col/nmt/wp and this call grew three parameters in three changes before the
-- pattern was obvious. Anything the marker needs to know about its subject goes on the
-- record now, and this signature stops moving.
function IqmCards:draw_beacon(i, ax, ay, ndx, ndy, ang, a, mtr, cfg, size, clamped, e)
	local b = self.beacons and self.beacons[i]
	if not b then return end
	local tex, col, nmt, ring = e.tex, e.col, e.nmt, e.ring
	b.hidden = false
	a = floor(a)
	if a > 255 then a = 255 elseif a < 0 then a = 0 end
	-- The accent unless the caller resolved a tint for this role (beacon_color; see
	-- BEACON_RGB). Markers took the accent and NOTHING else until R2.34, on the grounds
	-- that one is read at a glance, often against sky, and a dark tint would make it the
	-- least legible thing on screen. That objection was real and is now much weaker: since
	-- R2.31 every part of the badge carries a black keyline baked into its own texture, so
	-- a dark tint keeps its edge against sky and foliage instead of dissolving into them.
	-- It is still why this is opt-in and why the accent is the default and the fallback.
	--
	-- ...OR THE MARKER'S OWN COLOUR, in mode 3 (R2.62). One branch, HERE, is the whole
	-- feature: this line is the single place a marker reaches for the accent -- the glyph,
	-- the chevron, the waypoint ring, the metre readout and the name all take cr/cg/cb from
	-- it -- so substituting the triple here colours every part of every mark that is not
	-- wearing a resolved role colour, and does it without a second colour path to keep in
	-- step with this one.
	--
	-- The substitution is on the ACCENT, not on `col`: the resolve in iqm_core leaves mode 3
	-- entirely alone (it is one of the nils there), so the role and task-kind colours behave
	-- exactly as they do in mode 0 -- a selected mutant hunt keeps its lime reticle. Mode 3
	-- changes which gold the rest of the marks are, and nothing else.
	local cr, cg, cb = cfg.col_r, cfg.col_g, cfg.col_b
	if cfg.beacon_color == 3 then cr, cg, cb = cfg.beacon_r, cfg.beacon_g, cfg.beacon_b end
	if col then cr, cg, cb = col[1], col[2], col[3] end

	-- ---- content metrics ------------------------------------------------
	-- The readout is SPRITES now (R2.30), so its size is ours to choose rather than the
	-- engine's: RD_G.f of the glyph size, which means it finally follows "marker size"
	-- like the rest of the badge. Everything below is arithmetic on that one number --
	-- no measuring, no string building, and so nothing allocated per frame at all
	-- (the text path concatenated once per metre walked and asked the font for its
	-- metrics; both are gone).
	--
	-- Recompute the character list only when the metre count changes. Digits are pulled
	-- most-significant first straight into the widget slots, which is why this needs no
	-- buffer: b.gl[k].tex IS the list.
	local rh = mtr and max(2, size * RD_G.f) or 0        -- readout height, UI units
	local dw = rh * RD_G.w  * UI_KX                      -- a digit's drawn width...
	local mw = rh * RD_G.mw * UI_KX                      -- ...and the suffix's
	local trk, spg = rh * RD_G.trk * UI_KX, rh * RD_G.sp * UI_KX
	-- INTERFERENCE FOLDS INTO THIS CACHE KEY rather than sitting anywhere near the draw.
	-- NZ.ce is a counter iqm_noise bumps once a beat while the readout is corrupting, so a
	-- lying readout costs exactly the InitTexture that a changed metre count already costs,
	-- and nothing per frame. It is 0 and constant whenever the feature is at rest, so the
	-- test below is the same single compare it always was plus one more.
	local ce = NZ.ce
	if mtr and (b.m ~= mtr or b.ce ~= ce) then
		b.m, b.ce = mtr, ce
		local v = mtr; if v > 999 then v = 999 end       -- the waypoint has no range cap
		local n = 0
		local h100 = floor(v / 100)
		if h100 > 0 then n = n + 1; b.gl[n].t = DIGIT_TEX[h100] end
		if h100 > 0 or v >= 10 then n = n + 1; b.gl[n].t = DIGIT_TEX[floor(v / 10) % 10] end
		n = n + 1; b.gl[n].t = DIGIT_TEX[v % 10]
		b.nd = n                                         -- digits, before the suffix
		-- ...and then one of them is a lie. ONE, and never the metre suffix: the readout
		-- has to stay recognisable AS a range for the corruption to read as the number
		-- being wrong rather than as the badge drawing rubbish. Which digit moves is the
		-- band hash's own beat counter, so it is deterministic and a harness can assert on
		-- it -- and it changes place between beats instead of one digit flickering.
		if ce > 0 and n > 0 then
			local k = (ce % n) + 1
			b.gl[k].t = DIGIT_TEX[(ce * 7 + k * 3) % 10]
		end
		n = n + 1; b.gl[n].t = "iqm_digit_m"
		b.gn = n
	end
	local nd = mtr and (b.nd or 1) or 0
	-- total width: the digits, tracked, then the gap and the suffix
	local tw = mtr and (nd * dw + (nd - 1) * trk + spg + mw) or 0
	local th = mtr and rh or 0

	-- The ring's own K and the glyph's fit inside it, both 1 when there is no ring -- so an
	-- unringed marker lays out through these lines exactly as it did before them.
	local rgeom = ring and RING_GEOM[ring] or nil
	local rk    = ring and (rgeom and rgeom.k or RING_K) or 0
	local gfit  = (rgeom and rgeom.fit) or 1
	local iw = tex and max(2, floor(size * gfit * UI_KX + 0.5)) or 0
	local ih = tex and (size * gfit) or 0
	-- The ring's drawn box, sized from `size` rather than from the (already rounded) glyph
	-- so the two roundings do not compound -- and because the glyph is no longer the thing
	-- the ring is a multiple OF once fit is in play.
	--
	-- NAMED rgw/rgh AND NOT rw/rh, which is not a style preference: `rh` is already taken,
	-- forty lines up, by the RANGE READOUT's glyph height -- and `local rh` here shadowed
	-- it for the rest of the function. The readout's own width was computed before the
	-- shadow and its height after, so the digits came out at the RING's height against
	-- widths meant for the readout's: tall and thin on a ringed marker, and INVISIBLE on
	-- every other one, where this expression is 0. Both shipped to a screenshot. Nothing
	-- caught it because no harness draws a readout and a ring together; the source guard
	-- in the waypoint harness is what replaces that.
	local rgw = (ring and tex) and max(2, floor(size * rk * UI_KX + 0.5)) or 0
	local rgh = (ring and tex) and (size * rk) or 0
	-- How far the waypoint ring stands out past the glyph on each side, folded into the
	-- badge metrics rather than drawn over them. It has to be: the readout sits directly
	-- under the glyph with BADGE_GAP between them, so a ring sized off the glyph reaches
	-- BELOW the glyph and closes on the range text -- reported from play as the ring
	-- being too close to it. Shrinking RING_K alone would only have made the collision
	-- smaller; this removes it at any RING_K, and keeps the badge box honest about what
	-- is actually inside it (which the chevron's attachment point reads).
	local rox = (rgw > 0) and (rgw - iw) * 0.5 or 0
	local roy = (rgh > 0) and (rgh - ih) * 0.5 or 0
	local gap = (ih > 0 and th > 0) and BADGE_GAP or 0
	local bh  = ih + roy * 2 + gap + th + BADGE_PAD * 2
	-- Floor the width against the height. Sized purely to content, a short readout
	-- ("6 m") gives a tall narrow slab that reads as a UI panel someone left on the
	-- screen; a squarish chip reads as a marker. Tuning knob if it looks off.
	local bw  = max(max(iw + rox * 2, tw) + BADGE_PAD * 2, floor(bh * 0.7 + 0.5))

	-- ---- badge placement -------------------------------------------------
	-- Walk out from the badge centre to its own edge along the pointing direction,
	-- then sit the chevron just past that, so the two touch whichever way the
	-- chevron swings. Scaling the direction onto the rect boundary is the same trick
	-- the screen-edge clamp uses, one rectangle smaller.
	-- The chevron is drawn a little smaller than the glyph: at equal size it reads as
	-- a second marker competing with the badge rather than as the badge's tail.
	--
	-- With no chevron (the on-screen case, R2.30) there is nothing to make room for, so
	-- the badge sits ON the anchor point instead of backed off from it -- which is
	-- BEACON_ABOVE above the head, where the chevron used to hang.
	local cs = size * 0.75
	local reach = 0
	if clamped then
		local adx, ady = abs(ndx), abs(ndy)
		local edge = min((adx > 0.001) and (bw * 0.5 / adx) or 1e9,
		                 (ady > 0.001) and (bh * 0.5 / ady) or 1e9)
		reach = edge + cs * 0.32          -- slight overlap reads as attached, not adrift
	end
	local bcx, bcy = ax - ndx * reach, ay - ndy * reach
	local bl, bt = bcx - bw * 0.5, bcy - bh * 0.5

	-- ---- draw ------------------------------------------------------------
	if BADGE_PLATE then
		b.shadow:SetWndSize(S(bw + BADGE_SHM * 2, bh + BADGE_SHM * 2))
		b.shadow:SetWndPos(P(bl - BADGE_SHM + 2, bt - BADGE_SHM + 2))
		b.shadow:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(floor(95 * a / 255), 0, 0, 0))
		b.shadow:Show(true)

		b.plate:SetWndSize(S(bw, bh))
		b.plate:SetWndPos(P(bl, bt))
		b.plate:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(floor(180 * a / 255), 24, 22, 19))
		b.plate:Show(true)
	else
		b.shadow:Show(false)
		b.plate:Show(false)
	end

	-- The chevron is the "which way" half of the badge, and it only has an answer worth
	-- drawing when the marker is PARKED AT A SCREEN EDGE (R2.30): there it is the only
	-- thing saying where the NPC is. On-screen the badge is already sitting over their
	-- head, so an arrow pointing down at what it is attached to said nothing and just
	-- added a second mark to read.
	if clamped then
		-- chevron: rotated, so NO squeeze (see above)
		b.chev:SetWndSize(S(cs, cs))
		b.chev:SetWndPos(P(ax - cs * 0.5, ay - cs * 0.5))
		b.chev:EnableHeading(true)
		b.chev:SetHeading(ang)
		b.chev:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(a, cr, cg, cb))
		b.chev:Show(true)
	else
		b.chev:Show(false)
	end

	if tex then
		-- The marker's glyph flickers with the cards' (R2.59b). Folded into the SAME cache
		-- that already re-points this texture, keyed on the wanted glyph AND the corruption
		-- key, so a clean beat after a clean beat is one compare and no InitTexture.
		--
		-- A marker's glyph is always a role glyph, never a faction emblem, so the family is
		-- known and the tinted flag is not needed here.
		local ick = NZ.ick
		local want = (ick ~= 0) and (corrupt_icon(tex, ick, true) or tex) or tex
		if b.tex ~= want or b.ick ~= ick then
			b.tex, b.ick = want, ick
			b.icon:InitTexture(want)
		end
		-- unrotated, so squeeze (see above). The glyph sits `roy` below the badge's inner
		-- top so the ring around it has room inside the box rather than over its edge.
		local icon_y = bt + BADGE_PAD + roy
		b.icon:SetWndSize(S(iw, ih))
		b.icon:SetWndPos(P(bcx - iw * 0.5, icon_y))
		b.icon:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(a, cr, cg, cb))
		b.icon:Show(true)

		-- The ring, concentric with the GLYPH and not with the badge -- the badge is
		-- taller than the glyph (it holds the readout underneath), so centring on it
		-- would hang the ring low. With rox/roy in the metrics above, this lands the ring
		-- exactly on the badge's inner top and leaves the readout its full gap.
		--
		-- WHICH ring is the caller's word (R2.63, see BEACON_RING): the waypoint's, whose
		-- cardinal gaps are wide, or the selected task's, whose are narrow. (This said
		-- "diagonal arcs" against "cardinal ones" until R2.63a and was the last copy of a
		-- claim corrected in five other files: BOTH gap the cardinals, and the gap WIDTH is
		-- the difference -- which is what RING_GEOM above exists for.) Re-pointed through the
		-- same one-compare cache the glyph above uses, because a static's texture is set
		-- and not creatable on the draw path, and a marker changes ring about as often as
		-- it changes NPC -- so this is a compare per frame and an InitTexture almost never.
		-- No corruption pass: the interference envelope corrupts the GLYPH (the mark being
		-- read) and leaves the badge's furniture alone, and the ring is furniture.
		if ring then
			if b.rtex ~= ring then b.rtex = ring; b.ring:InitTexture(ring) end
			b.ring:SetWndSize(S(rgw, rgh))
			b.ring:SetWndPos(P(bcx - rgw * 0.5, icon_y + ih * 0.5 - rgh * 0.5))
			b.ring:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(a, cr, cg, cb))
			b.ring:Show(true)
		else
			b.ring:Show(false)
		end
	else
		b.icon:Show(false)
		b.ring:Show(false)
	end

	-- ---- the name band ---------------------------------------------------
	-- Above the badge, or BELOW it when the marker is parked against the top of the
	-- screen and there is no room -- the marker is at an edge exactly when it matters
	-- most, so a name clipped off the top there would fail in the one case it was
	-- turned on for.
	--
	-- Measured, not guessed: SetText + AdjustWidthToText + GetWidth, the same three calls
	-- draw_slot uses, and cached on the string so the four widgets are only measured when
	-- the marker changes NPC rather than every frame.
	if nmt then
		if b.nmt ~= nmt then
			b.nmt = nmt
			b.nm:SetText(nmt)
			b.nm_sh:SetText(nmt)
			b.nm:AdjustWidthToText()
			b.nm_sh:AdjustWidthToText()
			b.nmw = b.nm:GetWidth()
		end
		local nw = b.nmw
		-- centred on the badge, then kept on screen. Clamping the measured box rather
		-- than a fixed one is what stops a short name being shoved away from its own
		-- marker at the screen edge.
		local nx = bcx - nw * 0.5
		if nx < 4 then nx = 4 elseif nx + nw > 1020 then nx = 1020 - nw end
		local ny = bt - NAME_H - NAME_GAP
		if ny < 2 then ny = bt + bh + NAME_GAP end       -- no room above: hang it under
		b.nm_sh:SetWndPos(PS(nx + 1, ny + 1))
		b.nm_sh:SetTextColor(SHC(floor(a * 0.7)))
		b.nm_sh:Show(true)
		b.nm:SetWndPos(P(nx, ny))
		-- The MARKER'S OWN colour -- the accent, or whatever beacon_color resolved for
		-- this role -- so the name reads as part of the mark rather than as a label
		-- sitting near it. It first shipped in the cards' warm off-white, on the grounds
		-- that the glyph already carries the colour coding; asked for and changed, and
		-- the request is the better call: two elements of one marker in two colours is a
		-- distinction that means nothing to look at.
		--
		-- The catch, if a dark beacon_color ever reads badly here: the glyph survives a
		-- dark tint because R2.31 baked a keyline into its art, and TEXT CANNOT HAVE ONE.
		-- All the name has is the soft shadow copy above, so it is the one element on the
		-- marker where a dark faction colour (army, ISG) has less help than the rest.
		b.nm:SetTextColor(GetARGB(a, cr, cg, cb))
		b.nm:Show(true)
	else
		b.nm:Show(false)
		b.nm_sh:Show(false)
	end

	if not mtr then
		-- Bounded by the high-water mark, not by the pool (route-perf 4.7). With the range
		-- readout switched off this branch runs for every drawn beacon on every frame, and
		-- parking four already-parked widgets each time is the same shape ea3263b fixed in
		-- the minimap's hide_from -- and that draw_beacons' own `bhigh` already avoids one
		-- level up.
		for k = 1, b.gs or MAX_RGLYPH do b.gl[k].g:Show(false) end
		b.gs = 0
		return
	end
	-- Readout centred on the badge, under the glyph, laid out left to right. It never
	-- rotates: a range upside-down at the top of the screen would be unreadable.
	local gx = bcx - tw * 0.5
	-- ...clear of the ring when there is one: roy twice, once for the space above the
	-- glyph and once for the ring's reach below it.
	local gy = bt + BADGE_PAD + roy + ih + roy + gap
	for k = 1, b.gn do
		local e = b.gl[k]
		local suffix = (k > nd)
		local w = suffix and mw or dw
		local h = suffix and (rh * RD_G.mh) or rh
		local y = suffix and (gy + rh * RD_G.mdy) or gy
		if suffix then gx = gx + spg - trk end
		if e.tex ~= e.t then
			e.tex = e.t
			e.g:InitTexture(e.t)
		end
		e.g:SetWndSize(S(w, h))
		e.g:SetWndPos(P(gx, y))
		e.g:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(a, cr, cg, cb))
		e.g:Show(true)
		gx = gx + w + trk
	end
	-- ...and park only what was shown, exactly as the route marks' park loop does with its
	-- `prev` (route-perf 4.7). A steady readout re-parks nothing, and a readout that drops a
	-- digit -- 100 m to 99 m -- parks precisely the one glyph it dropped.
	for k = b.gn + 1, b.gs or MAX_RGLYPH do b.gl[k].g:Show(false) end
	b.gs = b.gn
end

function IqmCards:hide_slot(i)
	local s = self.slots[i]
	if not s then return end
	-- Tracked-but-invisible cards (out of range / occluded / PDA open) hit this
	-- every frame; once hidden, the 13 Show(false) calls below are pure waste, so
	-- they're skipped until draw_slot shows the slot again.
	if s.hidden then return end
	s.hidden = true
	s.shadow:Show(false)
	s.plate:Show(false)
	s.chip:Show(false)
	s.accent:Show(false)
	s.icon:Show(false)
	s.line_sh:Show(false)
	s.line:Show(false)
	s.glow:Show(false)
	s.node:Show(false)
	s.head:Show(false)
	s.name:Show(false)
	s.head_sh:Show(false)
	s.name_sh:Show(false)
end

function IqmCards:draw_slot(i, hx, hy, header, name, a, cfg, nscale, lift, icon, chip, cr, cg, cb, icon_tinted, tg)
	local s = self.slots[i]
	if not s then return end
	s.hidden = false
	a = floor(a)
	if a > 255 then a = 255 end
	nscale = nscale or 1
	lift   = lift or 0
	-- accent colour: per-card (faction colour when faction_colors is on), falling back
	-- to the shared gold accent so a nil never draws a black element.
	cr = cr or cfg.col_r; cg = cg or cfg.col_g; cb = cb or cfg.col_b
	chip   = (chip and icon) and true or false   -- chip mode needs a glyph to stand alone

	-- Ambient role cards render per the role_style option: a compact ICON-ONLY CHIP
	-- (no header/name, since the PDA legend already teaches the glyphs and quiet is the
	-- point of ambient cards), or the full text card with the glyph inlined before
	-- the text. State cards (and Tactical Compass absent) are always full text.
	local pad, accent_w = 9, 3
	local ch  = chip and 34 or 40    -- chip plate a little shorter than the text card
	-- glyph height: icon-only chips use the big size; the inline glyph on a text card
	-- (role glyph in "text + icon" mode, or a faction emblem) sits a little larger than
	-- the text so it reads clearly alongside it.
	local IH  = chip and 26 or 22
	-- squeeze the glyph's width by UI_KX like the node dot: the 4:3 virtual UI is
	-- stretched onto the real screen, so an equal w/h square would draw as an oval.
	local iw    = icon and max(2, floor(IH * UI_KX + 0.5)) or 0
	local ispan = (icon and not chip) and (iw + 6) or 0   -- inline glyph + gap before the text
	local lead  = (ispan > 0) and 4 or pad                -- gap from accent bar to first content (tighter when a glyph leads)

	-- card size. Strings / the glyph are fixed for the life of this slot's NPC, so
	-- the SetText/AdjustWidthToText/GetWidth measure (four text widgets) and the
	-- glyph's InitTexture run only when they actually change; otherwise the cached
	-- width is reused. This was previously paid on every frame.
	-- TWO CACHE PASSES, and they are separate for one reason: THE PLATE IS SIZED FROM THE
	-- MEASURED TEXT (cw, below), so if corruption went through the same pass it would resize
	-- the card on every corrupt beat -- the plate, the leader-line join and the drop shadow
	-- all breathing eleven times a second. That reads as the card being resized, not as the
	-- text glitching, and it fights the entrance rise.
	--
	-- So: the CLEAN pass owns `cw` and only runs when the real header, name, icon or chip
	-- mode changes. The CORRUPT pass re-points text and glyph and never touches `cw`. The
	-- lookalike table keeps the substituted string within a pixel of the clean one's width
	-- anyway, so the pinned plate is not visibly too wide for what is in it.
	local cw = s.cw
	local clean = header ~= s.last_head or name ~= s.last_name
	              or icon ~= s.last_icon or chip ~= s.last_chip
	if clean then
		if icon then s.icon:InitTexture(icon) end
		if chip then
			cw = iw + 18   -- chip: glyph centred with equal padding either side
		else
			s.head:SetText(header)
			s.name:SetText(name)
			s.head_sh:SetText(header)
			s.name_sh:SetText(name)
			s.head:AdjustWidthToText()
			s.name:AdjustWidthToText()
			s.head_sh:AdjustWidthToText()
			s.name_sh:AdjustWidthToText()
			-- floored so the plate seams (below) land on integer columns
			cw = floor(accent_w + lead + ispan + max(s.head:GetWidth(), s.name:GetWidth()) + pad + 0.5)
		end
		s.cw, s.last_head, s.last_name, s.last_icon, s.last_chip = cw, header, name, icon, chip
	end

	-- The corrupt pass. txk/ick are 0 on a clean beat and a seed on a corrupt one, so a run
	-- of clean beats compares equal and rebuilds nothing -- which is why iqm_noise publishes
	-- keys rather than booleans. Re-run after a clean change too, since that pass just wrote
	-- the true strings back over whatever was there.
	local txk, ick = NZ.txk, NZ.ick
	if clean or txk ~= s.last_txk then
		if not chip then
			-- The header only; the name is deliberately never corrupted (see LOOKALIKE).
			local ht = (txk ~= 0) and corrupt_text(header, txk) or header
			s.head:SetText(ht)
			s.head_sh:SetText(ht)
			-- Width is NOT re-measured and NOT re-adjusted: the widgets keep the clean text's
			-- box, so a substituted glyph draws inside the same rect the true one had.
		end
		s.last_txk = txk
	end
	if icon and (clean or ick ~= s.last_ick) then
		local it = (ick ~= 0) and corrupt_icon(icon, ick, icon_tinted) or icon
		s.icon:InitTexture(it or icon)
		s.last_ick = ick
	end

	-- place the card up and to one side of the head; flip left near the right edge.
	-- `lift` drops it a few px on first appearance so it rises into place as it fades in.
	local right = (hx + cfg.card_off_x + cw) <= 1014
	local cx    = right and (hx + cfg.card_off_x) or (hx - cfg.card_off_x - cw)
	if cx < 4 then cx = 4 end
	local cy = hy - cfg.card_off_y - ch + lift
	if cy < 4 then cy = 4 end

	-- whole-card soft drop shadow: a feathered slab a little larger than the plate,
	-- nudged down-right and tinted black, so the card reads as lifted off the world.
	-- Tighter on chips: at chip size the full margin reads as a dark blob.
	local sh_m, sh_ox, sh_oy = 6, 3, 4
	if chip then sh_m, sh_ox, sh_oy = 3, 2, 2 end
	s.shadow:SetWndPos(P(cx - sh_m + sh_ox, cy - sh_m + sh_oy))
	s.shadow:SetWndSize(S(cw + sh_m * 2, ch + sh_m * 2))
	s.shadow:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(floor(120 * a / 255), 0, 0, 0))
	s.shadow:Show(true)

	-- plate geometry. Both the chip and the text card are a SINGLE rounded-rect quad
	-- (chip uses the near-square iqm_chip, text card the wider iqm_plate). A 3-slice
	-- would put a seam at each cap join; at fractional UI scaling those round to a 1px
	-- gap or overlap and show as a thin line (transparent or dark) against the world.
	-- One quad has no seam. The corner radius stretches a little with width, which at
	-- these sizes is not noticeable.
	local pbg = chip and s.chip or s.plate
	pbg:SetWndPos(P(cx, cy)); pbg:SetWndSize(S(cw, ch))

	-- glyph: centred on the whole plate in chip mode, or inlined between the accent
	-- bar and the text. Tinted the accent colour, fading with the card.
	if icon then
		if chip then
			s.icon:SetWndPos(P(cx + floor((cw - iw) * 0.5), cy + floor((ch - IH) * 0.5)))
		else
			s.icon:SetWndPos(P(cx + accent_w + lead, cy + floor((ch - IH) * 0.5)))
		end
		s.icon:SetWndSize(S(iw, IH))
		-- role glyphs are white-on-transparent and tint to the accent colour; faction
		-- emblems carry their own colour, so they're drawn white (untinted), just faded.
		if icon_tinted == false then
			s.icon:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(a, 255, 255, 255))
		else
			s.icon:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(a, cr, cg, cb))
		end
		s.icon:Show(true)
	else
		s.icon:Show(false)
	end

	-- accent bar down the left edge, inset vertically clear of the rounded corners.
	-- Kept on chips too, since it's the card family's identity stripe.
	local acc_in = 7
	s.accent:SetWndPos(P(cx, cy + acc_in))
	s.accent:SetWndSize(S(accent_w, ch - acc_in * 2))

	if not chip then
		-- text, positioned absolutely (parented to self), starting past the accent bar
		-- (and the inline glyph, when there is one). Each line has a 1px black shadow
		-- copy one pixel down-right, drawn behind it.
		local txo = cx + accent_w + lead + ispan
		s.head_sh:SetWndPos(PS(txo + 1, cy + 6))
		s.head:SetWndPos(P(txo, cy + 5))
		s.name_sh:SetWndPos(PS(txo + 1, cy + 20))
		s.name:SetWndPos(P(txo, cy + 19))
	end

	-- Single round node at the head point, with a soft glow halo behind it. The node's
	-- dark border ring is baked into its texture (dark rim, white core), so it's one
	-- element and nothing can misalign. Size grows with proximity (nscale). The 4:3
	-- virtual UI is stretched onto the real (wider) screen, so an equal w/h square draws
	-- as an oval; we squeeze the width by UI_KX to keep both dot and halo round. Snapped.
	if cfg.show_node then
		local ns  = max(2, floor(cfg.node_size * nscale + 0.5))
		local nsx = max(2, floor(ns * UI_KX + 0.5))
		-- gentle breathing pulse on the glow halo (size +/-10%, alpha 75-100% of base),
		-- a slow ~1.6s sine so it draws the eye to the objective without being busy. The
		-- core dot stays crisp/steady. Time-based, so it's framerate-independent.
		local p_scale, p_alpha = 1, 1
		if cfg.node_pulse then
			local sn = sin(tg * 0.0039)
			p_scale = 1 + 0.10 * sn
			p_alpha = 0.875 + 0.125 * sn
		end
		local gs  = max(6, floor(cfg.node_size * nscale * 2.8 * p_scale + 0.5))
		local gsx = max(6, floor(gs * UI_KX + 0.5))
		s.glow:SetWndSize(S(gsx, gs))
		s.glow:SetWndPos(P(floor(hx - gsx * 0.5 + 0.5), floor(hy - gs * 0.5 + 0.5)))
		s.glow:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(floor(150 * a / 255 * p_alpha), cr, cg, cb))
		s.glow:Show(true)
		s.node:SetWndSize(S(nsx, ns))
		s.node:SetWndPos(P(floor(hx - nsx * 0.5 + 0.5), floor(hy - ns * 0.5 + 0.5)))
		s.node:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(a, cr, cg, cb))
		s.node:Show(true)
	else
		s.glow:Show(false)
		s.node:Show(false)
	end

	-- leader line: head point -> nearest bottom corner of the card. The strip
	-- rotates about its own centre, so put its centre on the segment midpoint.
	local corner_x = right and cx or (cx + cw)
	local corner_y = cy + ch
	local dx  = corner_x - hx
	local dy  = corner_y - hy
	-- extend the line a little past the corner so its end tucks under the card (the
	-- card is drawn over the line), giving a flush join with no gap at any aspect ratio
	local seglen = sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy)
	if seglen > 0.001 then
		local over = 6
		dx = dx + dx / seglen * over
		dy = dy + dy / seglen * over
		corner_x = hx + dx
		corner_y = hy + dy
	end
	-- The engine's heading rotation multiplies the strip's X by UI_KX (aspect
	-- correction) while plain SetWndPos (node/card) is not, so we pre-divide dx by
	-- UI_KX and derive length/angle from that: the rotated ends then land exactly
	-- on the head point and the card corner. Y is flipped (screen Y points down).
	local dxk = dx / UI_KX
	local len = sqrt(dxk * dxk + dy * dy)
	local ang = atan2(-dy, dxk)
	local mx  = (hx + corner_x) * 0.5
	local my  = (hy + corner_y) * 0.5
	local lx  = mx - len * 0.5
	local ly  = my - cfg.line_thick * 0.5

	-- outline halo: same strip, black, a touch thicker, sharing the line's exact
	-- centre and angle so it can never drift. It reads as a thin dark edge that keeps
	-- the line legible over bright backgrounds. Drawn behind the line.
	local halo_t = cfg.line_thick + LINE_HALO
	s.line_sh:SetWndSize(S(len, halo_t))
	s.line_sh:SetWndPos(PS(mx - len * 0.5, my - halo_t * 0.5))
	s.line_sh:EnableHeading(true)
	s.line_sh:SetHeading(ang)
	s.line_sh:SetTextureColor(SHC(floor(cfg.line_a * a / 255 * 0.6)))
	s.line_sh:Show(true)

	s.line:SetWndSize(S(len, cfg.line_thick))
	s.line:SetWndPos(P(lx, ly))
	s.line:EnableHeading(true)
	s.line:SetHeading(ang)

	if DEBUG then
		if tg - _dbg_t > 1000 then
			_dbg_t = tg
			printf("[IQM] slot=%s head=(%s,%s) card=(cx=%s cy=%s cw=%s ch=%s right=%s) corner=(%s,%s) len=%s a=%s",
				i, hx, hy, cx, cy, cw, ch, tostring(right), corner_x, corner_y, len, a)
		end
	end

	-- colours scaled by the eased alpha. Plate is a warm charcoal (not pure black)
	-- and the name is a warm off-white, so the card reads as one soft warm object
	-- rather than harsh black-and-white behind the accent. The header/name each get a
	-- black shadow copy at ~0.7 alpha for legibility over bright scenes.
	local plate_col = GetARGB(floor(cfg.card_a * a / 255), 24, 22, 19)
	if chip then
		s.chip:SetTextureColor(plate_col); s.chip:Show(true); s.plate:Show(false)
	else
		s.plate:SetTextureColor(plate_col); s.plate:Show(true); s.chip:Show(false)
	end
	s.line:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(floor(cfg.line_a * a / 255), cr, cg, cb))
	s.line:Show(true)

	s.accent:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(a, cr, cg, cb))
	s.accent:Show(true)

	if chip then
		-- chip mode: no text
		s.head_sh:Show(false)
		s.name_sh:Show(false)
		s.head:Show(false)
		s.name:Show(false)
	else
		local tsh = floor(a * 0.7)
		s.head_sh:SetTextColor(SHC(tsh))
		s.name_sh:SetTextColor(SHC(tsh))
		s.head:SetTextColor(GetARGB(a, cr, cg, cb))
		s.name:SetTextColor(GetARGB(a, 214, 210, 202))

		s.head_sh:Show(true)
		s.name_sh:Show(true)
		s.head:Show(true)
		s.name:Show(true)
	end
end


-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Lifecycle, config and the debug seam
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
--- Create the card dialog and attach it to the HUD, once. Returns the instance, or
--  nil while the HUD is not built yet.
--
--  get_hud() returns nil at on_game_start (the HUD isn't built then), so this is
--  driven lazily off the update loop rather than at startup. It lives with the class
--  because a constructor call belongs beside its class declaration; everything else
--  about when the overlay is up is iqm_core' business.
function ensure()
	if CARDS then return CARDS end
	local hud = get_hud()
	if not hud then return nil end
	CARDS = IqmCards()
	hud:AddDialogToRender(CARDS)
	return CARDS
end

--- The dialog, or nil if it has not been built yet. For anything that draws through
--  it without holding its own handle.
function get()
	return CARDS
end

--- Retint the live route, for the F7 colour cycler. Same reason the style has a setter:
--  a colour can only be judged on the ground it is drawn against, and going through the
--  menu for each candidate costs a reload of the route and the comparison with it.
--  Nothing is persisted -- apply_config puts the MCM's own values back.
function route_tint(r, g, b)
	if r then RTE.r, RTE.g, RTE.b = r, g, b end
	return RTE.r, RTE.g, RTE.b
end

--- Recompute the aspect correction the engine applies to rotated (heading) statics.
--
--  kx = (h/w) / (UI_BASE_HEIGHT/UI_BASE_WIDTH) = (h/w) / (768/1024). The engine
--  scales rotated-static X by this; we invert it so the leader line lines up.
--  Called on config change and, per frame, only when the resolution actually moves --
--  which is what _ui_w is remembering.
function update_kx(w, h)
	UI_KX = (w > 0 and h > 0) and ((h / w) / (768 / 1024)) or 1
end

--- Per-frame resolution watch, for the render path: recompute UI_KX only when the
--  device's width has actually changed. Costs one comparison on every other frame.
function kx_tick(dev)
	if dev and dev.width and dev.width > 0 and dev.width ~= _ui_w then
		_ui_w = dev.width
		update_kx(dev.width, dev.height)
	end
end

--- The live aspect correction, for the one drawing path that is not in this file
--  (iqm_beacon's screen-edge clamp). Hoisted into a function local there, once per
--  marker per frame; inside this file it stays the plain upvalue it always was.
function kx()
	return UI_KX
end

--- PULLED, not pushed: iqm_core calls this at the end of read_config and this file
--  reaches over for the look options. See the seam note at the top.
function apply_config()
	local C = iqm_core.config()
	DEBUG = C.debug_log and true or false
	-- Interference: bind the published state and the geometry that indexes it. THE TABLE,
	-- not a copy -- iqm_noise mutates it every frame and never rebinds it, so this is bound
	-- once and read forever. (The rebind off the rest table is the only one that happens;
	-- state() returns the same table for the life of the process.)
	NZ = iqm_noise.state()
	NZ_NB, NZ_INV_BH = iqm_noise.band_geom()
	FR_R, FR_G, FR_B = iqm_noise.fringe_rgb()
	-- Flatten the two glyph tables into indexable pools for corrupt_icon. Rebuilt here rather
	-- than at file scope because both are other modules' and only whole by on_game_start, and
	-- rebuilt on every config change rather than once because that is free and cannot go
	-- stale. Emptied first: apply_config runs again on every load, and appending would grow
	-- the pools without bound.
	--
	-- ipairs is wrong for both -- they are keyed by role and by faction, not by index -- so
	-- the order here is pairs order, which is arbitrary and does not matter: the pool is only
	-- ever indexed by a hash seed, and "arbitrary" is the requirement.
	ROLE_POOL, FACT_POOL = {}, {}
	local ri = iqm_beacon and iqm_beacon.beacon_icon
	if ri then for _, t in pairs(ri) do ROLE_POOL[#ROLE_POOL + 1] = t end end
	local ft = iqm_core.faction_tex and iqm_core.faction_tex()
	if ft then for _, t in pairs(ft) do FACT_POOL[#FACT_POOL + 1] = t end end
	-- SAID OUT LOUD, because the silence is what cost a session. Both of these tables are
	-- shipped by this mod and are never legitimately empty, so an empty pool is a broken
	-- invariant and not a soft dependency -- and every guard downstream is built to swallow
	-- it (corrupt_icon returns nil, the draw site falls back to the true glyph), which is
	-- right for the drawing and wrong for the diagnosis. R2.59b: faction_tex was declared
	-- above the local it returns, answered nil, and emblems silently never corrupted.
	--
	-- Not behind DEBUG. A player never sees this line and a broken build always should; it
	-- fires at most once per config change, not on any frame path.
	if #ROLE_POOL < 2 or #FACT_POOL < 2 then
		printf("[IQM] !! interference glyph pools came back short (roles=%s factions=%s) -- icon corruption is disabled; check the accessors' declaration ORDER",
			tostring(#ROLE_POOL), tostring(#FACT_POOL))
	end
	-- The readout is sprites, so its size is a plain number and belongs to the player
	-- rather than to the engine's font ladder. Stored as a percent of the glyph size.
	RD_G.f = C.beacon_rsize / 100
	-- The shape number is LOOKED UP rather than trusted, and the lookup is the whole
	-- check: RTE.SHAPES has holes in it since R2.43 cut five shapes off the menu, so
	-- "in range" says nothing. An ltx written before that cut still holds a 1, 2, 4, 5 or
	-- 6, and an MCM list can outlive the list it was written from in the other direction
	-- too (a downgrade). Either way an unresolved number would take RTE.SB to nil and
	-- every mark with it, so anything that is not a live shape falls back to the default.
	RTE.shape = C.route_shape
	if not RTE.SHAPES[RTE.shape] then RTE.shape = 7 end
	shape_box()                        -- fills RTE.SB; needed whether or not the UI exists
	if CARDS then CARDS:apply_shape() end
	RTE.a    = C.route_a
	-- 0..10 in the menu, 0..1 here, and it is a fraction of the colour's HEADROOM rather
	-- than of the colour (see draw_route). 1 means "as bright as this colour goes without
	-- shifting hue", so the ceiling is 1 and not 1.5 -- past 1 the brightest channel would
	-- pin and the hue would drift, which is the thing the headroom form exists to prevent.
	RTE.pulse = max(0, min(1, C.route_pulse * 0.1))
	RTE.r, RTE.g, RTE.b = C.route_r, C.route_g, C.route_b

	local dev = device()
	update_kx(dev and dev.width or 0, dev and dev.height or 0)
end
